      "The task gives three explicit sets, so before combining anything each one is written out in full and read one number at a time. $A=\\{1,2,3,4,5\\}$ is the list of the first five positive integers. $B=\\{3,4,5,6,7\\}$ is the list of five consecutive integers starting at $3$, and comparing it to $A$ shows that $3$, $4$, and $5$ are common to both lists while $1$ and $2$ belong only to $A$ and $6$, $7$ belong only to $B$. $C=\\{5,6,7,8,9\\}$ is the list of five consecutive integers starting at $5$; checking it against $A$ and $B$ shows $5$ is the only number all three sets share, $6$ and $7$ are shared by $B$ and $C$ but not $A$, and $8$, $9$ belong only to $C$.\n\nFour operations are needed to resolve the five statements, and each is defined before it is used. The **intersection** $X\\cap Y$ keeps only the numbers that appear in both $X$ and $Y$. The **union** $X\\cup Y$ keeps every number that appears in at least one of $X$ and $Y$, each number written once even if it appears in both. The **difference** $X\\setminus Y$ keeps the numbers of $X$ that do not also appear in $Y$. Two sets are **disjoint** exactly when their intersection is empty, meaning they share no number at all.\n\nComputing $A\\cap B$: scanning $A=\\{1,2,3,4,5\\}$ against $B=\\{3,4,5,6,7\\}$, the numbers $3$, $4$, $5$ appear in both, while $1$ and $2$ do not appear in $B$. Hence $A\\cap B=\\{3,4,5\\}$.\n\nComputing $A\\cup C$: every number that appears in $A$ or in $C$ (or both) must be listed once. From $A$ that is $1,2,3,4,5$, and from $C$ that is $5,6,7,8,9$, with $5$ counted only once since it is shared. Collecting all of them gives $A\\cup C=\\{1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9\\}$, a set of nine numbers running from $1$ to $9$.\n\nComputing $(A\\cap B)\\cap C$: using the intersection already found, $A\\cap B=\\{3,4,5\\}$, and intersecting that with $C=\\{5,6,7,8,9\\}$ keeps only the numbers present in both, which is just $5$ (since $3$ and $4$ are not in $C$). So $(A\\cap B)\\cap C=\\{5\\}$.\n\nComputing $A\\setminus C$: starting from $A=\\{1,2,3,4,5\\}$ and removing every number that also appears in $C=\\{5,6,7,8,9\\}$, only $5$ is removed (it is the sole overlap between $A$ and $C$, since $6,7,8,9$ never appear in $A$). The numbers $1,2,3,4$ have no match in $C$, so they remain. Hence $A\\setminus C=\\{1,2,3,4\\}$, a set with four elements.\n\nChecking disjointness of $B$ and $C$: $B=\\{3,4,5,6,7\\}$ and $C=\\{5,6,7,8,9\\}$ share the numbers $5$, $6$, and $7$, so $B\\cap C=\\{5,6,7\\}$, which is not empty. Therefore $B$ and $C$ are not disjoint.\n\nWith these five results in hand, $A\\cap B=\\{3,4,5\\}$, $A\\cup C=\\{1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9\\}$, $(A\\cap B)\\cap C=\\{5\\}$, $A\\setminus C=\\{1,2,3,4\\}$, and $B\\cap C=\\{5,6,7\\}\\ne\\emptyset$, each of the five statements A through E can be checked directly against the matching computation above.",
