      "The context gives a single set $A=\\{a,b,c\\}$ containing exactly three named objects: $a$, $b$, and $c$. Before checking any statement, it helps to fix two different relations that are easy to confuse. **Membership**, written $x\\in A$, means $x$ is literally one of the three listed objects, $a$, $b$, or $c$. **Subsethood**, written $S\\subseteq A$, means every object inside $S$ is also one of those three objects; $S$ itself does not need to be one of the three listed objects, it only needs its contents to come from that list.\n\nThe **power set** $\\mathcal P(A)$ is defined as the collection of every possible subset of $A$, including the empty set and $A$ itself. Since $A$ has three objects and each one can independently be included in or left out of a candidate subset, there are $2\\times2\\times2=2^3=8$ ways to build a subset, so $\\mathcal P(A)$ has exactly $8$ elements. Writing them all out confirms the count directly:\n\n$$\\mathcal P(A)=\\{\\emptyset,\\{a\\},\\{b\\},\\{c\\},\\{a,b\\},\\{a,c\\},\\{b,c\\},\\{a,b,c\\}\\}.$$\n\nThat list has eight entries, matching the count from $2^3$.\n\nLooking specifically at $\\{a,b\\}$: it is one of the eight entries in $\\mathcal P(A)$, so it is a valid subset of $A$, but the elements of $A$ are only $a$, $b$, and $c$ themselves, individual letters, not pairs of letters. Since $\\{a,b\\}$ is not one of those three listed objects, $\\{a,b\\}\\notin A$, even though $\\{a,b\\}\\subseteq A$.\n\nLooking at the empty set $\\emptyset$: the subset test asks whether every element of $\\emptyset$ also lies in $A$, and since $\\emptyset$ has no elements at all, there is nothing that could fail this test; the condition holds automatically (**vacuously**). So $\\emptyset\\subseteq A$, which also matches the fact that $\\emptyset$ appears in the power set listed above.\n\nLooking at whether $A$ is a proper subset of itself: a **proper subset** relationship $S\\subsetneq A$ requires two things simultaneously, that $S\\subseteq A$ (which is true reflexively for $S=A$) and that $S\\ne A$. The second requirement can never hold when $S=A$, since a set is always equal to itself, so $A$ is never a proper subset of $A$, regardless of what $A$ contains.\n\nLooking at the two-element subsets: from the power set listed above, the entries with exactly two elements are $\\{a,b\\}$, $\\{a,c\\}$, and $\\{b,c\\}$, exactly three of them. This matches choosing $2$ objects out of $3$ to include, which counts as $\\binom{3}{2}=\\frac{3!}{2!\\cdot1!}=3$.\n\nWith the full power set written out, the vacuous truth of $\\emptyset\\subseteq A$, the impossibility of proper self-inclusion, and the count of two-element subsets all established, each of the five statements can now be matched directly against these facts.",
