      "The context gives two explicit sets, $E=\\{1,3,5,7\\}$ and $F=\\{3,4,5,6\\}$. Comparing their elements one at a time: $1$ appears only in $E$; $3$ appears in both $E$ and $F$; $5$ appears in both $E$ and $F$; $7$ appears only in $E$; $4$ appears only in $F$; $6$ appears only in $F$. So the two sets share exactly the two numbers $3$ and $5$, meaning $E\\cap F=\\{3,5\\}$, while $1,7$ belong only to $E$ and $4,6$ belong only to $F$.\n\nThe **difference** $X\\setminus Y$ is defined as the elements of $X$ that are not also elements of $Y$, so it removes exactly the shared part $X\\cap Y$ from $X$ and keeps the rest of $X$ untouched.\n\nComputing $E\\setminus F$: starting from $E=\\{1,3,5,7\\}$ and removing the shared numbers $3$ and $5$ (since both lie in $F$) leaves $1$ and $7$, which are the elements of $E$ with no match in $F$. So $E\\setminus F=\\{1,7\\}$.\n\nComputing $F\\setminus E$: starting from $F=\\{3,4,5,6\\}$ and removing the same shared numbers $3$ and $5$ (since both lie in $E$) leaves $4$ and $6$. So $F\\setminus E=\\{4,6\\}$.\n\nComparing the two differences: $E\\setminus F=\\{1,7\\}$ and $F\\setminus E=\\{4,6\\}$ contain no common numbers, and in fact $1\\in E\\setminus F$ while $1\\notin F\\setminus E$ already shows the two sets are unequal. In general, $E\\setminus F$ only ever contains elements of $E$ and $F\\setminus E$ only ever contains elements of $F$, and since $E$ and $F$ are not identical here, the two one-sided differences describe different regions and are not equal to each other.\n\nComputing the union of the two differences: joining every number that appears in $E\\setminus F=\\{1,7\\}$ or in $F\\setminus E=\\{4,6\\}$ gives $\\{1,4,6,7\\}$, listing all four numbers once each.\n\nComputing the intersection of the two differences: a number in $E\\setminus F$ must, by definition, be absent from $F$, while a number in $F\\setminus E$ must, by definition, be present in $F$. No number can simultaneously be absent from $F$ and present in $F$, so these two sets can never share a member; $(E\\setminus F)\\cap(F\\setminus E)=\\emptyset$ for any two sets $E$ and $F$, and it holds here in particular.\n\nWith $E\\setminus F=\\{1,7\\}$, $F\\setminus E=\\{4,6\\}$, their union $\\{1,4,6,7\\}$, and their intersection $\\emptyset$ all computed directly, every one of the five statements can now be checked against these results.",
