    difficulty_level: "2/5",
    sort_order: 7,
    solution_overview:
      'The context gives four numbers about a cohort of students. The total cohort size is $50$ students. $30$ students take Mathematics, so $|M|=30$. $25$ students take Economics, so $|E|=25$. The two courses share $12$ students who take both, so $|M\\cap E|=12$; this last number is the key piece that keeps the other two totals from simply being added together, since those $12$ students are counted inside both the $30$ and the $25$.\n\nTo find how many students take at least one of the two courses, the **inclusion-exclusion principle** is used: adding $|M|$ and $|E|$ directly would count each of the $12$ double-takers twice, once inside the Mathematics count and once inside the Economics count, so that overcount must be subtracted back out exactly once:\n\n$$|M\\cup E|=|M|+|E|-|M\\cap E|=30+25-12=43.$$\n\nSo $43$ students take Mathematics, Economics, or both.\n\nSince the cohort itself has $50$ students total, and the union just computed accounts for everyone who takes at least one of the two courses, the students taking **neither** course are the remainder: $50-43=7$.\n\nThe students taking **only** Mathematics (Mathematics but not Economics) are found by starting from the full Mathematics count and removing those who are also in Economics, since those overlap students should not be counted as "only Mathematics": $|M\\setminus E|=|M|-|M\\cap E|=30-12=18$.\n\nFor completeness, the same logic gives the only-Economics count as $|E\\setminus M|=|E|-|M\\cap E|=25-12=13$, a number that becomes relevant when checking whether $E\\subseteq M$: since $13$ students take Economics without also taking Mathematics, these $13$ students are members of $E$ that are not members of $M$, which is a direct violation of what $E\\subseteq M$ would require (every member of $E$ also being a member of $M$).\n\nFinally, the given overlap $|M\\cap E|=12$ is itself nonzero, and two sets are disjoint precisely when their intersection is empty; since $12\\ne 0$, the sets $M$ and $E$ share students and are not disjoint.\n\nWith $|M\\cup E|=43$, the neither-count $=7$, $|M\\setminus E|=18$, $|E\\setminus M|=13$ witnessing that $E\\not\\subseteq M$, and $|M\\cap E|=12\\ne 0$ ruling out disjointness, each of the five statements can now be checked directly against these computed numbers.',
