    difficulty_level: "2/5",
    sort_order: 8,
    solution_overview:
      'The context defines a universal set $U=\\{1,2,\\ldots,9\\}$ split into three named blocks: $A=\\{1,2,3\\}$, $B=\\{4,5,6\\}$, and $C=\\{7,8,9\\}$. Looking at how these blocks are built, each one occupies a separate third of $U$\'s nine numbers with no numbers repeated across blocks: $A$ takes the first three, $B$ takes the middle three, and $C$ takes the last three.\n\nChecking **pairwise disjointness** first, meaning every pair of the three blocks shares nothing: comparing $A=\\{1,2,3\\}$ with $B=\\{4,5,6\\}$, no number appears in both lists, so $A\\cap B=\\emptyset$; comparing $A$ with $C=\\{7,8,9\\}$, likewise no shared number, so $A\\cap C=\\emptyset$; comparing $B$ with $C$, again nothing shared, so $B\\cap C=\\emptyset$. All three pairwise intersections are empty, which is exactly the definition of pairwise disjoint sets, so $A$, $B$, $C$ are pairwise disjoint.\n\nChecking the **triple intersection** $A\\cap B\\cap C$: since $A\\cap B$ is already empty, intersecting that empty result further with $C$ can only stay empty (there is nothing left in $A\\cap B$ that could also be checked against $C$). So $A\\cap B\\cap C=\\emptyset$ as well.\n\nChecking whether $\\{A,B,C\\}$ forms a **partition** of $U$: a partition requires three conditions together, that each block is nonempty, that the blocks are pairwise disjoint, and that their union recovers all of $U$. Each block has three elements, so none is empty. Pairwise disjointness was just confirmed above. For the union, collecting every number in $A$, $B$, or $C$ gives $\\{1,2,3\\}\\cup\\{4,5,6\\}\\cup\\{7,8,9\\}=\\{1,2,\\ldots,9\\}=U$, so the union condition holds too. All three conditions being satisfied means $\\{A,B,C\\}$ is indeed a partition of $U$.\n\nChecking the **difference** $A\\setminus B$: this keeps the elements of $A$ that are not also in $B$. Since $A\\cap B=\\emptyset$ was already shown, no element of $A$ needs to be removed at all, so $A\\setminus B=A=\\{1,2,3\\}$, which is not empty. This shows that an empty intersection between $A$ and $B$ makes $A\\setminus B$ equal to the whole of $A$, not equal to $\\emptyset$.\n\nChecking whether an empty intersection forces one of the two sets to be empty: the claim "$A\\cap B=\\emptyset$ implies $A=\\emptyset$ or $B=\\emptyset$" is a general logical claim about any two sets, and it can be tested directly using $A$ and $B$ themselves as a counterexample. Here $A\\cap B=\\emptyset$ is true, yet $A=\\{1,2,3\\}$ has three elements and $B=\\{4,5,6\\}$ has three elements, so neither is empty. A single case where the hypothesis holds but the conclusion fails is enough to disprove a general "implies" claim, so an empty intersection does not force either set to be empty.\n\nWith pairwise disjointness confirmed, the triple intersection empty, the partition conditions all verified, $A\\setminus B=\\{1,2,3\\}\\ne\\emptyset$, and the counterexample $A,B$ themselves disproving the last general claim, every one of the five statements can now be resolved directly.',
