      "**D.** → False\n\nThis statement claims $R=\\{\\{1,2\\},\\{3,4\\},\\{5\\}\\}$ is a partition of $A$. The three blocks are nonempty and pairwise disjoint, but their union is $\\{1,2\\}\\cup\\{3,4\\}\\cup\\{5\\}=\\{1,2,3,4,5\\}$, which does not include $6$. Since $6\\in A$ is left uncovered, the full-coverage condition fails, so $R$ is not a partition of $A$, and the statement is false.",
      "**E.** → True\n\nThis statement claims any set with $n\\ge2$ elements always has more than one valid partition. For any such set, the single-block collection containing just the whole set is always a valid one-block partition, and the collection of all singletons is always a valid $n$-block partition. When $n\\ge2$, these two constructions are different from each other (a $1$-block collection cannot equal an $n$-block collection once $n\\ge2$), so at least two distinct valid partitions always exist, and the statement is true.",
