    sort_order: 11,
    solution_overview:
      "The context gives $A=\\{1,2,3,4,5,6\\}$, six elements, and explicitly defines what a **partition** of a set is: a collection of blocks that are (i) all nonempty, (ii) pairwise disjoint (no two blocks share an element), and (iii) together cover every element of $A$ (their union equals $A$). All three of these conditions must hold simultaneously for a collection to count as a partition; failing even one disqualifies it.\n\nChecking $P=\\{\\{1,2\\},\\{3,4\\},\\{5,6\\}\\}$ against the three conditions: each of the three blocks $\\{1,2\\}$, $\\{3,4\\}$, $\\{5,6\\}$ is nonempty, satisfying (i). Comparing the blocks pairwise, $\\{1,2\\}\\cap\\{3,4\\}=\\emptyset$, $\\{1,2\\}\\cap\\{5,6\\}=\\emptyset$, and $\\{3,4\\}\\cap\\{5,6\\}=\\emptyset$, since each block uses numbers no other block uses, satisfying (ii). Their union is $\\{1,2\\}\\cup\\{3,4\\}\\cup\\{5,6\\}=\\{1,2,3,4,5,6\\}=A$, satisfying (iii). All three conditions hold, so $P$ is a valid partition of $A$.\n\nChecking $Q=\\{\\{1,2,3\\},\\{3,4,5,6\\}\\}$: the two blocks are both nonempty, satisfying (i). But comparing them pairwise, $\\{1,2,3\\}\\cap\\{3,4,5,6\\}=\\{3\\}$, which is not empty, since the number $3$ appears in both blocks. Condition (ii), pairwise disjointness, fails immediately, so $Q$ cannot be a partition regardless of whether the union condition would have worked out; a single shared element is enough to disqualify it.\n\nChecking $R=\\{\\{1,2\\},\\{3,4\\},\\{5\\}\\}$: the three blocks are nonempty, satisfying (i), and pairwise disjoint, since $\\{1,2\\}$, $\\{3,4\\}$, $\\{5\\}$ share no numbers with each other, satisfying (ii). But their union is $\\{1,2\\}\\cup\\{3,4\\}\\cup\\{5\\}=\\{1,2,3,4,5\\}$, which is missing the number $6$ from $A$. Condition (iii), full coverage, fails, so $R$ is not a partition, even though it passed the first two conditions.\n\nChecking whether the number of blocks in a partition is forced to equal $|A|=6$: this is a general claim, and it can be tested with two concrete constructions on $A$ itself. The single block $\\{A\\}$, meaning the one-block collection containing $A$ as its only member, is itself nonempty, trivially pairwise disjoint (there is only one block, so there is no other block to compare it to), and its union is just $A$; so $\\{A\\}$ is a valid one-block partition. Separately, the collection of six singletons, $\\{\\{1\\},\\{2\\},\\{3\\},\\{4\\},\\{5\\},\\{6\\}\\}$, has six nonempty blocks, pairwise disjoint since each singleton uses a different number, and their union recovers all of $A$; so this is a valid six-block partition. Since a valid partition exists with $1$ block and another valid partition exists with $6$ blocks on the very same set $A$, the number of blocks is clearly not fixed at $|A|=6$; it can be smaller.\n\nThat same pair of constructions, the one-block partition $\\{A\\}$ and the all-singletons partition, works for any set with at least two elements: for any $n\\ge2$, both constructions are valid partitions of that set, and they are different from each other whenever $n\\ge2$ (since a one-block collection cannot equal an $n$-block collection when $n\\ge2$). So a set with $n\\ge2$ elements always has at least two different valid partitions, meaning more than one partition always exists.\n\nWith $P$ confirmed a valid partition, $Q$ disqualified by failing pairwise disjointness (rule ii), $R$ disqualified by failing full coverage (rule iii), the one-block and six-block partitions both shown valid on $A$, and the same two-construction argument extended to any $n\\ge2$, every one of the five statements can now be resolved directly.",
