    sort_order: 14,
    solution_overview:
      "The survey gives seven numbers about $150$ tourists and three museums: the count in each single museum, $|A|=80$, $|B|=70$, $|C|=60$; the count in each pair of museums, $|A\\cap B|=30$, $|B\\cap C|=25$, $|A\\cap C|=20$; and the count in all three museums at once, $|A\\cap B\\cap C|=10$. Each of these seven numbers plays a specific role in the inclusion exclusion formula for three sets, so it helps to name that role before using any of them.\n\n**Finding the size of the union.** Adding the three single counts, $80+70+60=210$, counts every tourist who visited exactly one museum once, every tourist who visited exactly two museums twice, and every tourist who visited all three museums three times, because that tourist is included once in each of the three totals. To correct the double counting, subtract each pairwise total once: $210-30-25-20=135$. This subtraction removes the tourists in exactly two museums down to being counted once, as intended, but it also removes the ten tourists who visited all three museums three separate times, once for each pair they belong to, leaving them counted zero times overall. To fix that, the triple count is added back once: $135+10=145$. So the full formula reads\n\n$$|A\\cup B\\cup C|=80+70+60-30-25-20+10=145,$$\n\nmeaning $145$ of the $150$ tourists visited at least one of the three museums.\n\n**Finding how many visited none.** Since the survey covers $150$ tourists in total and $145$ of them are accounted for by the union, the remaining $150-145=5$ tourists visited none of the three museums.\n\n**Finding the exactly-two regions.** The given pairwise counts like $|A\\cap B|=30$ include the ten tourists who visited all three museums, since those ten tourists are automatically part of every pair they belong to. To find the number who visited exactly A and B and not C, the ten triple visitors must be removed from that pairwise total: $30-10=20$. The same logic applies to the other two pairs: exactly B and C only is $25-10=15$, and exactly A and C only is $20-10=10$.\n\n**Finding how many visited only Museum A.** Starting from the $80$ tourists who visited Museum A at all, remove the tourists who also visited Museum B, $30$ of them, and the tourists who also visited Museum C, $20$ of them. But the ten tourists who visited all three museums are members of both of those removed groups, so subtracting both $30$ and $20$ removes them twice, and they must be added back once to correct for that:\n\n$$80-30-20+10=40.$$\n\nSo exactly $40$ tourists visited Museum A and no other museum, not $30$; the number $30$ obtained without adding the triple back undercounts by exactly the size of the triple group.\n\n**Finding how many visited at least two museums.** This group is made of the three exactly-two regions found above, together with the tourists in all three museums: $20+15+10+10=55$. Every one of the five statements can now be checked directly against these established numbers: $145$ for the union, $5$ for none, $20$ for exactly A and B, $40$ for only A, and $55$ for at least two museums.",
