      "**C.** → False\n\nThis statement claims that the very same rule $f(n)=2n$ also sets up a one-to-one correspondence between $N$ and the odd natural numbers. But for any natural number $n$, the output $2n$ is, by construction, exactly twice an integer, which makes it an even number every single time; it can never equal an odd number such as $1$, $3$, or $5$. So $f$ does not reach a single odd number, meaning it fails to be onto the set of odd naturals, and it certainly cannot be a bijection onto them (the correct rule for the odds would instead be $g(n)=2n-1$, which is not what is being claimed here). Because the claimed target is wrong, the statement is false.",
      "**D.** → True\n\nThis statement claims that the pair of facts already established, $E\\subsetneq N$ together with $|E|=|N|$, is exactly the phenomenon that breaks the finite-set intuition that a proper subset must be strictly smaller. That is precisely what was shown: $E$ is genuinely a proper subset of $N$, since it misses $1,3,5,\\ldots$, yet the bijection $f(n)=2n$ proves the two infinite sets have identical cardinality. Having both a strict inclusion and an equal size at once is impossible for finite sets, so this pair is a legitimate counterexample to the finite-set slogan, and the statement is true.",
