    difficulty_level: "3/5",
    sort_order: 15,
    solution_overview:
      "Begin with what is actually given. $N=\\{1,2,3,\\ldots\\}$ is the full list of natural numbers, continuing forever with no last number. $E=\\{2,4,6,\\ldots\\}$ is the list of even natural numbers, also continuing forever with no last even number. Every number written in $E$'s list ($2,4,6,\\ldots$) is itself one of the numbers written in $N$'s list, so right away $E\\subseteq N$. But $N$'s list also contains $1,3,5,\\ldots$, the odd naturals, and none of those appear in $E$'s list. In particular $1\\in N$ while $1\\notin E$, so $E$ and $N$ are not the same set: the inclusion $E\\subseteq N$ is proper, written $E\\subsetneq N$. That settles statement A as true and gives the key fact $E\\subsetneq N$ that the rest of the task turns on.\n\nNext ask whether being a proper subset forces $E$ to have fewer elements than $N$, the way it would for a finite set (a finite set can never be matched one-to-one with a proper subset of itself). Since both $N$ and $E$ are infinite, this finite-set intuition needs to be checked, not assumed. To check it, look at the pairing rule $f(n)=2n$. Test whether $f$ is one-to-one: if $f(n_1)=f(n_2)$ then $2n_1=2n_2$, so $n_1=n_2$; different naturals never collide under $f$. Test whether $f$ is onto $E$: every even natural number can be written as $2k$ for some natural $k$, so every element of $E$ is hit by $f$. Being both one-to-one and onto makes $f$ a **bijection** from $N$ to $E$, and a bijection between two sets means they have the same cardinality, $|N|=|E|$. So $E$ is a proper subset of $N$ and yet has exactly as many elements as $N$: statement B, which claims proper subset must mean strictly fewer elements, is false, and statement D, which says this exact pair of facts ($E\\subsetneq N$ together with $|E|=|N|$) shows the finite-set slogan does not carry over to infinite sets, is true.\n\nNow examine the same formula $f(n)=2n$ under a different target: does it pair $N$ with the odd naturals instead? Every output $2n$ is, by construction, an even number, since it is twice an integer. No matter which natural $n$ is plugged in, $f(n)$ can never land on an odd number such as $1$, $3$, or $5$. So $f$ never reaches a single element of the odd naturals, meaning it is not onto that set at all, let alone a bijection onto it (the correct formula for the odds would be $g(n)=2n-1$, which is not what is being claimed). This makes statement C false.\n\nFinally, consider whether every infinite subset of $N$ must in fact be all of $N$. $E$ itself is the counterexample sitting right in the context: it is infinite, since after any even number $2n$ there is always a next one, $2n+2$, so its list never terminates; it is a subset of $N$; and yet it omits every odd number $1,3,5,\\ldots$, so $E\\ne N$. One infinite proper subset is enough to make the universal claim false, so statement E is false.",
