    solution_overview:
      "Start from exactly what is given: $A=\\{2,4,6,8,10,12\\}$ is a set built from six specific even numbers, and nothing else belongs to it. The entire task hinges on keeping straight two different relations that both use the numbers in $A$: **membership** (is a single object one of those six numbers?) and **subset** (is a whole collection made only of those six numbers?).\n\n**Membership checks.** For statement A, the question is whether $6$ itself, a bare number, is one of the six listed numbers. Scanning the list $2,4,6,8,10,12$, the number $6$ is indeed present, so $6\\in A$ is true. For statement B, the question is different in kind: it asks whether $\\{6\\}$, the *set* whose only member is $6$, is one of the six listed numbers. It is not, because every one of the six listed objects is a bare number, never a set; $\\{6\\}$ is simply not among $2,4,6,8,10,12$. So $\\{6\\}\\notin A$. It is worth noting explicitly what *is* true about $\\{6\\}$: since its only member, $6$, is in $A$, it is correct that $\\{6\\}\\subseteq A$ as a subset. But subset and membership are separate relations, and the statement under test asks specifically about membership, which fails.\n\n**Subset checks.** For statement C, $\\{6,8\\}$ is a subset of $A$ exactly when every member of $\\{6,8\\}$ is also a member of $A$. Both $6$ and $8$ are on the list defining $A$, so both membership tests pass, and $\\{6,8\\}\\subseteq A$. For statement D, the empty set $\\emptyset$ is being tested as a subset of $A$: the subset test asks whether every member of $\\emptyset$ is in $A$, but $\\emptyset$ has no members whatsoever, so there is nothing to check and the condition holds automatically (a vacuous truth). This means $\\emptyset\\subseteq A$ for the same reason it is a subset of every set.\n\n**Counting subsets.** For statement E, the number of subsets of a set with $n$ elements is $2^n$, since each of the $n$ elements can independently be placed inside or left outside any candidate subset, giving $2$ choices per element and $2\\times2\\times\\cdots\\times2$ ($n$ times) total combinations. Here $n=6$, so $A$ has $2^6=64$ subsets in total. A **proper** subset is defined as any subset that is not equal to the whole set, so the one subset that must be excluded from that count is $A$ itself, leaving $64-1=63$ proper subsets, exactly matching the statement.",
