      "**A.** → True\n\nThis statement claims that $A$ and $B$ are the same set. As established, solving $x^2-5x+6=0$ over the integers gives exactly the two roots $x=2$ and $x=3$, so $A=\\{2,3\\}$. The context defines $B=\\{2,3\\}$ directly. Comparing the two, both sets contain precisely the numbers $2$ and $3$ and nothing else, so every member of one is a member of the other in both directions, which is exactly what set equality requires. Hence $A=B$ and the statement is true.",
      "**B.** → True\n\nThis statement claims that $3$ belongs to $A$. Membership in $A$ requires being an integer solution of $x^2-5x+6=0$. Plugging in $x=3$: $3^2-5(3)+6=9-15+6=0$, so the equation is satisfied, and $3$ is indeed an integer. Both conditions defining $A$ are met by $3$, so $3\\in A$, making the statement true.",
