    solution_overview:
      "Start from the given definitions. $A$ is the set of integer solutions to $x^2-5x+6=0$, and $B$ is given directly as $\\{2,3\\}$. To know what $A$ actually contains, the quadratic must be solved first, before any of the statements can be checked.\n\n**Solving the quadratic.** Factor the expression: $x^2-5x+6=(x-2)(x-3)$. A product of two factors is zero exactly when at least one of the factors is zero, so $(x-2)(x-3)=0$ holds precisely when $x=2$ or $x=3$. Both of these values are integers, so both qualify for membership in $A$, giving $A=\\{2,3\\}$. There is no reason to keep one root and discard the other: the factorization treats them symmetrically, and either one alone makes the whole product vanish.\n\n**Comparing $A$ and $B$.** With $A=\\{2,3\\}$ and $B=\\{2,3\\}$ given, the two sets list exactly the same members, so $A=B$. Since $A$ has two distinct elements, $|A|=2$. Because $3$ is one of the two roots and an integer, $3\\in A$ directly.\n\n**Building $C$ from the same roots.** $C$ restricts attention to natural-number roots of the identical equation that also satisfy the extra condition $x>2$. The roots are still $2$ and $3$, both natural numbers, so the equation part of the definition changes nothing from $A$. Applying $x>2$ as an additional filter: $2$ fails this test since $2$ is not strictly greater than $2$, so $2$ is dropped; $3$ passes since $3>2$, so $3$ is kept. Only one value survives the filter, giving $C=\\{3\\}$.\n\nWith $A=\\{2,3\\}=B$, $|A|=2$, $3\\in A$, the false claim that $A=\\{2\\}$ ruled out by the symmetric factorization, and $C=\\{3\\}$ all confirmed directly from the same factoring step, every one of the five statements is now settled.",
