    answer_key: [false, true, false, true, true],
    tactical_explanations: [
      "**A.** → False\n\nThis statement claims that every element of $A$ is also an element of $B$. $A=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R:1<x<5\\}$ requires only $1<x<5$, while $B=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R:x\\ge3\\}$ requires $x\\ge3$; these are different conditions, so a number could satisfy the first without satisfying the second. Testing $x=2$: it satisfies $1<2<5$, so $2\\in A$, but $2\\ge3$ is false, so $2\\notin B$. Since $2$ is in $A$ but not in $B$, the subset relation fails, so $A\\subseteq B$ does not hold and the statement is false.",
      "**B.** → True\n\nThis statement claims that $A\\cap B$ equals the interval $[3,5)$. Belonging to $A\\cap B$ requires satisfying both conditions at once: $1<x<5$ from $A$'s definition, and $x\\ge3$ from $B$'s definition. Combining the two, the lower bound is raised from $1$ to $3$ (since $x\\ge3$ is the stricter requirement on that side, and $3$ itself does satisfy $1<3<5$, so $3$ is included), while the upper bound stays at $5$ but excluded, exactly as $A$ already required. This gives $A\\cap B=[3,5)$, matching the claim, so the statement is true.",
      "**C.** → False\n\nThis statement claims that every element of $B$ is also an element of $A$. $B=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R:x\\ge3\\}$ places no upper bound on $x$ at all, while $A$ requires $x<5$ strictly. Testing $x=10$: it satisfies $10\\ge3$, so $10\\in B$, but $10<5$ is false, so $10\\notin A$. Since $10$ is in $B$ but not in $A$, the reverse subset relation fails, so $B\\subseteq A$ does not hold and the statement is false.",
      "**D.** → True\n\nThis statement claims that $A\\cup B$ equals the interval $(1,\\infty)$. Belonging to $A\\cup B$ requires satisfying at least one of the two conditions, $1<x<5$ or $x\\ge3$. For any $x$ strictly greater than $1$: if $x<5$ as well then $A$'s condition is met directly, and if $x\\ge5$ then $B$'s condition $x\\ge3$ is automatically met since $5\\ge3$; either way, every $x>1$ satisfies at least one of the two conditions. No number at or below $1$ can satisfy either condition, since $A$ requires $x>1$ strictly and $B$ requires $x\\ge3$, which is even stricter. So exactly the numbers greater than $1$ qualify, giving $A\\cup B=(1,\\infty)$, matching the claim, so the statement is true.",
      "**E.** → True\n\nThis statement claims that some real number belongs to $A$ but not to $B$. The set difference $A\\setminus B$ consists of numbers satisfying $1<x<5$ while failing $x\\ge3$, meaning $1<x<3$, which is the nonempty interval $(1,3)$. A concrete witness is $x=2$: it satisfies $1<2<5$, so $2\\in A$, and it satisfies $2<3$, so $2\\not\\ge3$, meaning $2\\notin B$. Since such a number exists, in fact infinitely many of them, the statement is true.",
    ],
    difficulty_level: "1/5",
    sort_order: 3,
    solution_overview:
      "The context defines two sets by inequalities on the real numbers: $A=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R:1<x<5\\}$, which in interval notation is the open interval $(1,5)$, and $B=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R:x\\ge3\\}$, which is the closed-below, unbounded-above interval $[3,\\infty)$. Every statement in this task asks about a combination or comparison of these two conditions, so translating each into interval notation up front makes every remaining question a matter of comparing endpoints.\n\n**Testing $A\\subseteq B$.** This would require every number satisfying $1<x<5$ to also satisfy $x\\ge3$. But numbers between $1$ and $3$, such as $x=2$, satisfy $A$'s condition ($1<2<5$) while failing $B$'s condition ($2<3$), so this inclusion does not hold.\n\n**Testing $B\\subseteq A$.** This would require every number satisfying $x\\ge3$ to also satisfy $1<x<5$. But large numbers, such as $x=10$, satisfy $B$'s condition ($10\\ge3$) while failing $A$'s upper bound ($10\\not<5$), so this inclusion does not hold either. Neither direction of inclusion holds, so $A$ and $B$ are simply two overlapping intervals, neither containing the other.\n\n**Computing $A\\cap B$.** A number needs to satisfy both $1<x<5$ and $x\\ge3$ simultaneously. Combining the two lower bounds, $x>1$ and $x\\ge3$, the stricter one, $x\\ge3$, wins, and $3$ itself is a valid value since it also satisfies $1<3<5$; so the left endpoint of the intersection is $3$, included. The upper bound is untouched by $B$'s condition, so it stays at $x<5$, excluded. This gives $A\\cap B=[3,5)$.\n\n**Computing $A\\cup B$.** A number needs to satisfy at least one of the two conditions. Every number greater than $1$ falls into one of two cases: if it is also less than $5$, $A$'s condition covers it directly; if it is $5$ or more, then it is certainly at least $3$, so $B$'s condition covers it instead. So every $x>1$ is covered by the union. Numbers at or below $1$ satisfy neither condition, since both $A$'s lower bound and $B$'s lower bound sit above $1$. Hence $A\\cup B=(1,\\infty)$.\n\n**Locating $A\\setminus B$.** Numbers in $A$ but excluded from $B$ satisfy $1<x<5$ while failing $x\\ge3$, which narrows down to $1<x<3$, the interval $(1,3)$. This interval is nonempty, containing for instance $x=2$, so a real number satisfying \"in $A$ but not in $B$\" genuinely exists.\n\nWith $A=(1,5)$, $B=[3,\\infty)$, $A\\cap B=[3,5)$, $A\\cup B=(1,\\infty)$, and the witness numbers $2$ and $10$ all pinned down explicitly, every one of the five statements is now settled directly by comparison against these computed intervals.",
