    title: "Sales rep to client account coverage",
    subsection: "1.2",
    context:
      'A consulting firm has 5 sales reps and 8 client accounts. A "coverage assignment" is an ordered pair (rep, account) meaning that rep is the point of contact for that account. The firm wants to know how many distinct coverage assignments are possible, and how the count changes if the team grows.',
    statements: [
      "The total number of possible (rep, account) pairs is 40.",
      "The pair (Maria, Account 3) is the same coverage assignment as (Account 3, Maria).",
      "If the firm currently has zero client accounts, no coverage assignments can be formed no matter how many reps it hires.",
      'In the pair (rep, account), the statement "(Maria, Account 3) belongs to Reps $\\times$ Accounts" means Maria $\\in$ Accounts and Account $3 \\in$ Reps.',
      "If the firm hires one more rep (6 reps total) while keeping 8 accounts, the number of possible assignments becomes 48.",
    ],
    answer_key: [true, false, true, false, true],
    tactical_explanations: [
      "**A.** → True\n\nThis statement claims that there are $40$ possible (rep, account) coverage assignments in total. The firm has $5$ sales reps and $8$ client accounts, and a coverage assignment pairs one rep with one account, so this is exactly the Cartesian product $\\text{Reps}\\times\\text{Accounts}$. Every one of the $5$ reps can be paired independently with any of the $8$ accounts, giving $5\\times8=40$ distinct ordered pairs in total. Since this matches the claimed count, the statement is true.",
      "**B.** → False\n\nThis statement claims that (Maria, Account 3) is the same coverage assignment as (Account 3, Maria). A coverage assignment is defined as an *ordered* pair (rep, account), where the roles of the two coordinates are fixed: the first slot must name the rep and the second slot must name the account. (Maria, Account 3) correctly places a rep first and an account second, matching the intended meaning, while (Account 3, Maria) reverses that, placing an account name where a rep should be and a rep name where an account should be. Ordered pairs are only equal when both coordinates match in the same positions, and here the two coordinates are simply swapped, so the two pairs are different objects with different meanings, making the statement false.",
      "**C.** → True\n\nThis statement claims that with zero client accounts, no coverage assignments can be formed regardless of how many reps the firm hires. A coverage assignment requires choosing one account to pair with a rep, but if the pool of accounts is empty, there is nothing available to fill that second slot no matter how large the pool of reps grows. Using the product rule, the total number of assignments would be $r\\times0=0$ for any number of reps $r$, since multiplying by zero always gives zero. Since no assignment can ever be completed without an account to assign, the statement is true.",
      "**D.** → False\n\nThis statement claims that belonging to $\\text{Reps}\\times\\text{Accounts}$ means the *first* coordinate (Maria) must be an accounts and the *second* coordinate (Account 3) must be a rep, exactly reversing the actual rule. By definition, an ordered pair $(r,a)$ belongs to $\\text{Reps}\\times\\text{Accounts}$ precisely when its first coordinate $r$ is a member of $\\text{Reps}$ and its second coordinate $a$ is a member of $\\text{Accounts}$, in that order. So the correct reading of (Maria, Account 3) belonging to that product is that Maria must be in Reps (which she is, since she is named as a sales rep) and Account 3 must be in Accounts (which it is). The claim inverts both memberships, so it is false.",
      "**E.** → True\n\nThis statement claims that hiring one more rep, bringing the total to $6$ reps while keeping $8$ accounts, raises the number of possible assignments to $48$. Applying the same product rule as in statement A but with $6$ reps instead of $5$ gives $6\\times8=48$ possible (rep, account) pairs. This can also be seen by noting that the original $40$ pairs (from the $5$ existing reps) still exist, and the new sixth rep contributes $8$ additional pairs, one for each of the $8$ accounts, giving $40+8=48$ in total, consistent with the direct multiplication. Since this matches the claimed count, the statement is true.",
    ],
    difficulty_level: "1/5",
    sort_order: 5,
    solution_overview:
      "The context establishes that a coverage assignment is defined as an ordered pair (rep, account), meaning a specific rep is designated as the point of contact for a specific account, and that the firm currently has $5$ sales reps and $8$ client accounts. Because the assignment is an *ordered* pair drawn from two named collections, it is exactly the mathematical structure of a Cartesian product $\\text{Reps}\\times\\text{Accounts}$, where the first coordinate must always be a rep and the second coordinate must always be an account.\n\n**Counting the pairs.** For each of the $5$ reps, there are $8$ possible accounts that rep could be paired with, and these choices are independent of one another, so by the product rule the total number of ordered pairs is $5\\times8=40$. This is the total number of possible coverage assignments right now.\n\n**What order means here.** Since the pair is ordered, (Maria, Account 3) and (Account 3, Maria) are not interchangeable: the first slot of a coverage assignment must always name a rep, and the second slot must always name an account. (Maria, Account 3) respects that structure, since Maria is a rep and Account 3 is an account, but (Account 3, Maria) places an account name in the rep slot and a rep name in the account slot, breaking the intended structure entirely; these are two different pairs representing two different (and in the second case, meaningless) assignments. The same order rule governs the meaning of membership in the product: writing $(r,a)\\in\\text{Reps}\\times\\text{Accounts}$ specifically asserts that $r$, the first coordinate, is a rep, and $a$, the second coordinate, is an account, never the other way around.\n\n**Shrinking the account pool to zero.** The product rule multiplies the size of the rep pool by the size of the account pool. If the account pool has zero members, the total becomes $r\\times0$ for whatever number of reps $r$ the firm has, and multiplying anything by zero always yields zero. So no coverage assignment can exist without at least one account available to be the second coordinate, regardless of how many reps are hired.\n\n**Growing the rep pool by one.** Hiring a sixth rep changes the count from $5\\times8=40$ to $6\\times8=48$. This increase of $8$ makes sense directly: the new rep can be paired with each of the $8$ existing accounts exactly once, contributing $8$ brand-new pairs on top of the original $40$.\n\nWith the product rule giving $40$ current assignments, $48$ assignments after hiring one more rep, $0$ assignments whenever the account pool is empty, and the ordered-pair structure clarifying both which coordinate must be a rep and which must be an account, every one of the five statements is now directly resolvable.",
