      "**B.** → True\n\nThis statement claims $A\\cap B=\\{30,40,50\\}$. Checking each number against both lists, only $30$, $40$, and $50$ appear in both $A=\\{10,20,30,40,50\\}$ and $B=\\{30,40,50,60\\}$, while $10$ and $20$ live only in $A$ and $60$ only in $B$. So the intersection is exactly $\\{30,40,50\\}$, matching the statement, which is true.",
      "**C.** → True\n\nThis statement claims $A\\setminus B=\\{10,20\\}$, the numbers of $A$ not shared with $B$. Removing the three shared numbers $30$, $40$, and $50$ from $A=\\{10,20,30,40,50\\}$ leaves exactly $\\{10,20\\}$, as claimed. The statement is true.",
      "**D.** → False\n\nThis statement claims $B\\setminus A=A\\setminus B$. Here $A\\setminus B=\\{10,20\\}$ has two members while $B\\setminus A=\\{60\\}$ has only one member. Sets of different sizes can never be equal, so the two differences are certainly not the same set; the direction of subtraction genuinely changes the answer here. The statement is false.",
      "**E.** → True\n\nThis statement claims that $A$ and $C$ are disjoint. Every member of $A=\\{10,20,30,40,50\\}$ is at least $10$, while every member of $C=\\{1,2,3\\}$ is at most $3$, so no number can satisfy both conditions and appear in both lists. Since $A\\cap C=\\emptyset$, the two sets are disjoint by definition, and the statement is true.",
