    difficulty_level: "2/5",
    sort_order: 9,
    solution_overview:
      "Three explicit lists are given: $A=\\{10,20,30,40,50\\}$, the multiples of ten from $10$ through $50$; $B=\\{30,40,50,60\\}$, the multiples of ten from $30$ through $60$; and $C=\\{1,2,3\\}$, three small integers.\n\nComparing $A$ and $B$ number by number: $10$ appears only in $A$. $20$ appears only in $A$. $30$ appears in both. $40$ appears in both. $50$ appears in both. $60$ appears only in $B$. Tagging every number this way answers the first four statements directly.\n\n| Number | in $A$ | in $B$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 10 | yes | no |\n| 20 | yes | no |\n| 30 | yes | yes |\n| 40 | yes | yes |\n| 50 | yes | yes |\n| 60 | no | yes |\n\n**The intersection** keeps the rows marked yes in both columns, giving $A\\cap B=\\{30,40,50\\}$.\n\n**The union** keeps every row, since each number belongs to at least one of the two sets, giving $A\\cup B=\\{10,20,30,40,50,60\\}$.\n\n**The difference $A\\setminus B$** keeps the rows marked yes for $A$ and no for $B$, namely $10$ and $20$, so $A\\setminus B=\\{10,20\\}$.\n\n**The reverse difference $B\\setminus A$** keeps the rows marked no for $A$ and yes for $B$, only $60$, so $B\\setminus A=\\{60\\}$. Comparing sizes alone already shows these cannot be equal: $A\\setminus B$ has two members while $B\\setminus A$ has just one.\n\nFinally, $C=\\{1,2,3\\}$ never appears in the table at all, because none of $1$, $2$, or $3$ is a multiple of ten, so none of them can belong to $A$. This means $A\\cap C=\\emptyset$, and an empty intersection is precisely what makes two sets **disjoint**.\n\nWith $A\\cup B=\\{10,20,30,40,50,60\\}$, $A\\cap B=\\{30,40,50\\}$, $A\\setminus B=\\{10,20\\}$, $B\\setminus A=\\{60\\}$, and the disjointness of $A$ and $C$ all pinned down, every statement can now be checked against these results.",
