    solution_overview:
      "Three letter sets are given: $A=\\{a,b,c,d\\}$, $B=\\{c,d,e\\}$, and $C=\\{x,y\\}$.\n\nComparing $A$ and $B$ letter by letter: $a$ appears only in $A$. $b$ appears only in $A$. $c$ appears in both $A$ and $B$. $d$ appears in both $A$ and $B$. $e$ appears only in $B$. This single comparison is enough to answer the first four statements.\n\n**The intersection** $A\\cap B$ keeps the letters found in both lists, which are $c$ and $d$, so $A\\cap B=\\{c,d\\}$.\n\n**The union** $A\\cup B$ keeps every letter appearing in either list, written once: the four letters of $A$ plus the one new letter $e$ from $B$, giving $A\\cup B=\\{a,b,c,d,e\\}$.\n\n**The difference $A\\setminus B$** keeps only the letters of $A$ not also in $B$. Removing $c$ and $d$ from $A=\\{a,b,c,d\\}$ leaves $A\\setminus B=\\{a,b\\}$.\n\n**The reverse difference $B\\setminus A$** keeps only the letters of $B$ not also in $A$. Removing $c$ and $d$ from $B=\\{c,d,e\\}$ leaves $B\\setminus A=\\{e\\}$. Comparing $\\{a,b\\}$ against $\\{e\\}$, the two sets share nothing at all, so they are not equal.\n\nFinally, $C=\\{x,y\\}$ is compared against $A=\\{a,b,c,d\\}$: neither $x$ nor $y$ appears anywhere in $A$'s list, so $A\\cap C=\\emptyset$, meaning $A$ and $C$ are **disjoint**.\n\nWith $A\\cup B=\\{a,b,c,d,e\\}$, $A\\cap B=\\{c,d\\}$, $A\\setminus B=\\{a,b\\}$, $B\\setminus A=\\{e\\}$, and the disjointness of $A$ and $C$ established, every statement can now be verified directly.",
