    solution_overview:
      "The set $A=\\{m,n,p\\}$ has three elements and the set $B=\\{1,2\\}$ has two elements. The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ is defined as the set of every ordered pair whose first entry comes from $A$ and whose second entry comes from $B$, so building it means pairing each of the three letters with each of the two numbers, always keeping the letter in the first position.\n\n**Listing every pair explicitly.** Pairing $m$ with each number of $B$ gives $(m,1)$ and $(m,2)$. Pairing $n$ with each number of $B$ gives $(n,1)$ and $(n,2)$. Pairing $p$ with each number of $B$ gives $(p,1)$ and $(p,2)$. Collecting all six pairs gives\n\n$$A\\times B=\\{(m,1),(m,2),(n,1),(n,2),(p,1),(p,2)\\}.$$\n\n**Counting them.** There are three choices for the first entry and two choices for the second entry, and each choice of first entry can be combined with either choice of second entry independently, so the total count is $3\\times2=6$. This matches the general product rule for Cartesian products, $|A\\times B|=|A|\\cdot|B|$, here $3\\cdot2=6$.\n\n**Checking what ordered means.** A pair like $(m,1)$ belongs to $A\\times B$ precisely because its first entry $m$ comes from $A$ and its second entry $1$ comes from $B$. Reversing the order to $(1,m)$ puts the number first and the letter second, and since $A\\times B$ only accepts letter-first pairs, $(1,m)$ is not a member of $A\\times B$ even though it uses the same two objects. That reversed pair does belong to the other Cartesian product, $B\\times A$, which pairs each number of $B$ first with each letter of $A$ second:\n\n$$B\\times A=\\{(1,m),(1,n),(1,p),(2,m),(2,n),(2,p)\\}.$$\n\n**Comparing the two products.** $A\\times B$ and $B\\times A$ contain six pairs each, so they have the same size, $|A\\times B|=|B\\times A|=6$. But comparing them element by element shows they share no pair at all: every pair in $A\\times B$ starts with a letter, and every pair in $B\\times A$ starts with a number, so the two sets are equal in size but not equal as sets. Both of these established sets are enough to settle every one of the five statements directly by comparison.",
