    answer_key: [true, true, true, true, false],
    tactical_explanations: [
      "**A.** → True\n\nThis statement claims $A\\setminus B=\\{1,9\\}$. As sorted below, the numbers of $A=\\{1,3,5,7,9\\}$ not also in $B$ are $1$ and $9$, since $3$, $5$, and $7$ are shared with $B$ and must be excluded. This gives $A\\setminus B=\\{1,9\\}$ exactly as claimed, so the statement is true.",
      "**B.** → True\n\nThis statement claims $B\\setminus A=\\{11,13\\}$. From $B=\\{3,5,7,11,13\\}$, removing the shared numbers $3$, $5$, and $7$ leaves exactly $11$ and $13$, the numbers belonging to $B$ alone. This matches the statement, which is true.",
      "**C.** → True\n\nThis statement claims $A\\triangle B=\\{1,9,11,13\\}$. The symmetric difference joins the numbers exclusive to $A$, which are $\\{1,9\\}$, with the numbers exclusive to $B$, which are $\\{11,13\\}$, giving $\\{1,9,11,13\\}$. Checking each of these four numbers, every one belongs to exactly one of the two original sets, confirming the claimed set is correct. The statement is true.",
      "**D.** → True\n\nThis statement claims $(A\\setminus B)\\cap(B\\setminus A)=\\emptyset$. Structurally, anything in $A\\setminus B$ is by definition outside $B$, while anything in $B\\setminus A$ is by definition inside $B$, so no single number can satisfy both conditions at once, forcing the intersection to be empty for any two sets, not just this pair. The actual lists confirm it directly: $\\{1,9\\}$ and $\\{11,13\\}$ share no number. The statement is true.",
      "**E.** → False\n\nThis statement claims $A\\triangle B=A\\cup B$. The union keeps the shared bucket $\\{3,5,7\\}$ in addition to the exclusive numbers, giving $A\\cup B=\\{1,3,5,7,9,11,13\\}$, seven numbers, while the symmetric difference discards that shared bucket entirely, giving $A\\triangle B=\\{1,9,11,13\\}$, only four numbers. Since $3$, $5$, and $7$ appear in the union but not in the symmetric difference, the two sets are not equal. These two operations coincide only when the original sets share nothing at all, which is not the case here since $A\\cap B=\\{3,5,7\\}$ is not empty. The statement is false.",
    ],
    difficulty_level: "3/5",
    sort_order: 16,
    solution_overview:
      "Two sets of odd numbers are given: $A=\\{1,3,5,7,9\\}$ and $B=\\{3,5,7,11,13\\}$. Comparing them element by element: the numbers $3$, $5$, and $7$ appear in both lists; the numbers $1$ and $9$ appear only in $A$; and the numbers $11$ and $13$ appear only in $B$. Sorting every number this way produces three buckets:\n\n| Bucket | Meaning | Numbers |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Only in $A$ | this is $A\\setminus B$ | $1,\\ 9$ |\n| In both | this is $A\\cap B$ | $3,\\ 5,\\ 7$ |\n| Only in $B$ | this is $B\\setminus A$ | $11,\\ 13$ |\n\n**The two one-way differences.** $A\\setminus B$ keeps only what $A$ has that $B$ does not, which is the first bucket: $A\\setminus B=\\{1,9\\}$. $B\\setminus A$ keeps only what $B$ has that $A$ does not, the third bucket: $B\\setminus A=\\{11,13\\}$.\n\n**The symmetric difference.** $A\\triangle B$ is defined as the numbers belonging to exactly one of the two sets, that is, the first and third buckets joined together, leaving out the middle bucket of shared numbers entirely: $A\\triangle B=\\{1,9\\}\\cup\\{11,13\\}=\\{1,9,11,13\\}$.\n\n**The intersection of the two differences.** Every number in $A\\setminus B$ is, by definition, outside $B$, while every number in $B\\setminus A$ is, by definition, inside $B$; no number can be simultaneously outside and inside $B$, so these two sets can never share a member. Checking the actual lists confirms it: $\\{1,9\\}$ and $\\{11,13\\}$ have nothing in common, so $(A\\setminus B)\\cap(B\\setminus A)=\\emptyset$.\n\n**Comparing to the union.** The union $A\\cup B$ keeps all three buckets, including the shared middle one: $A\\cup B=\\{1,3,5,7,9,11,13\\}$, seven numbers. The symmetric difference $A\\triangle B=\\{1,9,11,13\\}$, four numbers, deliberately throws the shared bucket $\\{3,5,7\\}$ away. Since that middle bucket is not empty here, the union and the symmetric difference are genuinely different sets, differing by exactly those three shared numbers.\n\nWith the three buckets identified and $A\\setminus B$, $B\\setminus A$, $A\\triangle B$, and $A\\cup B$ all computed explicitly, every one of the five statements can now be checked.",
