    difficulty_level: "4/5",
    sort_order: 18,
    solution_overview:
      'The problem hands over four numbers describing a games club, and each one needs to be unpacked before combining. There are **40 students** total in the club; this is the whole population every other count must fit inside. Of these, **22 play chess**, forming a set $A$ of chess players; on its own this says nothing about who else those 22 students are or what else they play. Separately, **15 play checkers**, forming a set $B$ of checkers players. Finally, **6 students play both games**, meaning these 6 are already counted once among the 22 chess players and a second time among the 15 checkers players; they are not a separate group of people outside those two counts.\n\n**Peeling the overlap apart.** Because the 6 who play both are hiding inside the 22, subtracting them out gives the number who play chess and chess only: $22-6=16$. By the same logic, subtracting the 6 from the 15 gives the number who play checkers and checkers only: $15-6=9$.\n\n**Counting everyone who plays at least one game.** Adding the three genuinely distinct groups, chess-only (16), both (6), and checkers-only (9), gives everyone who plays something: $16+6+9=31$. This is exactly what the **inclusion-exclusion rule** computes directly, without needing to split into the three groups first:\n\n$$\\lvert A\\cup B\\rvert=\\lvert A\\rvert+\\lvert B\\rvert-\\lvert A\\cap B\\rvert=22+15-6=31.$$\n\n**Accounting for the rest of the club.** With 31 of the 40 students placed inside the union, the remaining $40-31=9$ students play neither game. Every member is now placed into one of four groups, and they total correctly: $16+6+9+9=40$.\n\n**Comparing the intersection to the union.** Every student who plays both games is, automatically, also a student who plays at least one game, since "both" is a stronger condition than "at least one". This means the set of both-game players is always **contained inside** the set of at-least-one-game players, so its size can never exceed the union\'s size. Here that means $6$ can never be larger than $31$, and indeed $6<31$.\n\nWith the club split into chess-only (16), both (6), checkers-only (9), and neither (9), and the containment relationship between $A\\cap B$ and $A\\cup B$ established, every one of the five statements can now be checked directly.',
