    tactical_explanations: [
      "**A.** → True\n\nThis statement claims Applicant P satisfies both of the bank's required conditions. Checking P's numbers, credit score 750 and ratio 35%, against the requirement: $750\\ge700$ holds, and $35\\%<40\\%$ holds. Both halves of the \"and\" requirement succeed for P, so P does satisfy the full two-part requirement, and the statement is true.",
      '**B.** → False\n\nThis statement claims that P\'s loan will definitely be approved. The bank\'s rule reads "approved **only if**" the requirement is met, which makes the requirement a necessary hurdle, not a guarantee. Statement A confirmed P clears that hurdle, but clearing a necessary condition never implies the outcome follows automatically; the rule supplies no arrow running from "meets the requirement" to "loan approved". Since satisfying a necessary condition does not entail the conclusion, nothing here forces approval, so the statement is false.',
      '**C.** → True\n\nThis statement claims Applicant Q does not satisfy both required conditions. Checking Q\'s numbers, credit score 720 and ratio 45%, against the requirement: $720\\ge700$ holds, but $45\\%<40\\%$ fails, since 45% is not below 40%. Because the requirement joins its two conditions with "and", one failing half, the ratio test, is enough to break the whole requirement. So Q does not satisfy the full two-part requirement, and the statement is true.',
      "**D.** → True\n\nThis statement claims that since Q fails one required condition, Q's loan will not be approved. This follows from reading the bank's rule as its contrapositive: the original rule says loan approved $\\Rightarrow$ requirement met, and its logically equivalent contrapositive says requirement not met $\\Rightarrow$ loan not approved. Statement C already established that Q fails the ratio half of the requirement, so the requirement is not met, and the contrapositive then guarantees Q's loan cannot be approved. The statement is true.",
      "**E.** → False\n\nThis statement claims that a debt-to-income ratio below 40% alone guarantees approval, regardless of credit score. Two separate problems break this claim. First, a low ratio is only one of the two conditions in the bank's requirement; the credit score test is a separate, independent condition that this statement ignores entirely. Second, even satisfying the complete two-part requirement, both conditions together, was already shown in statement B not to guarantee approval, since the rule only establishes the requirement as necessary, not sufficient. Since one satisfied condition guarantees even less than the full requirement does, and the full requirement guarantees nothing on its own, the statement is false.",
    ],
    difficulty_level: "1/5",
    sort_order: 6,
    solution_overview:
      'The context gives one rule and two applicants\' numbers, and each piece needs to be pinned down before any conclusion is drawn.\n\n**The bank\'s rule.** A loan is approved only if two conditions both hold at once: the credit score is at least 700, and the debt-to-income ratio is below 40%. The phrase "only if" makes this two-part requirement a **necessary** condition for approval, in symbols, loan approved $\\Rightarrow$ score $\\ge700$ and ratio $<40\\%$. Critically, "only if" does not say the reverse: meeting the requirement does not by itself force approval. The rule never states an arrow running the other way, so the two-part requirement is necessary but its sufficiency is left completely open.\n\n**Applicant P\'s numbers.** Credit score 750 and debt-to-income ratio 35%. Checking each half of the requirement: $750\\ge700$ holds, and $35\\%<40\\%$ holds. Both halves succeed, so Applicant P satisfies the full two-part requirement.\n\n**Applicant Q\'s numbers.** Credit score 720 and debt-to-income ratio 45%. Checking each half: $720\\ge700$ holds, but $45\\%<40\\%$ does not hold, since 45% is not below 40%. Because the requirement joins its two halves with "and", a single failing half, the ratio test here, is enough to break the whole requirement. So Applicant Q does not satisfy the full two-part requirement.\n\n**Reading the rule in reverse (the contrapositive).** The arrow loan approved $\\Rightarrow$ requirement met is logically equivalent to its contrapositive, requirement not met $\\Rightarrow$ loan not approved (negating and swapping both sides of an implication always preserves its truth value). This reverse reading is just as reliable as the original rule, and it is the one that applies directly to Applicant Q: since Q fails the requirement, Q\'s loan cannot be approved.\n\n**What the rule does not say about Applicant P.** Meeting the two-part requirement, as P does, only clears the necessary hurdle; the bank never promised that clearing it guarantees a yes. Nothing in the stated rule supplies the reverse arrow requirement met $\\Rightarrow$ loan approved, so P\'s fate beyond "eligible to be considered" cannot be determined from the given information.\n\n**A one-sided condition is not the same as the full requirement.** The full two-part requirement demands both the score test and the ratio test together. Satisfying only the ratio test, a low debt-to-income ratio without regard to the score, is just one of the two necessary halves, and even the complete two-part requirement was shown above not to guarantee approval on its own; a single half of it guarantees even less.\n\nWith P confirmed to meet both parts of the requirement, Q confirmed to fail one part (and therefore denied by the contrapositive), and the necessary-but-not-sufficient nature of the requirement established in both directions, all five statements below are directly resolvable.',
