    difficulty_level: "1/5",
    sort_order: 8,
    solution_overview:
      'The context gives one diagnostic criterion in two parts and describes two patients, and each piece should be unpacked individually first.\n\n**The necessary part of the criterion.** A patient is diagnosed with condition X only if they exhibit both symptom A and symptom B. Reading "only if" correctly: diagnosis with X $\\Rightarrow$ both symptom A and symptom B present. This makes having both symptoms together a **necessary** condition for the diagnosis; nobody is diagnosed without both symptoms being present first.\n\n**The explicitly denied sufficiency.** The context goes on to say that exhibiting both symptoms does not guarantee the diagnosis, because other conditions must also be ruled out first. This directly blocks the reverse arrow, both symptom A and symptom B present $\\Rightarrow$ diagnosis with X; that reverse arrow is explicitly false. So having both symptoms is necessary for the diagnosis, but not sufficient on its own.\n\n**Patient R\'s situation.** Patient R has been diagnosed with condition X. Applying the necessary-condition arrow, diagnosis with X $\\Rightarrow$ both symptom A and symptom B present, to Patient R\'s diagnosis shows that Patient R must exhibit both symptom A and symptom B; there is no room in the criterion for a diagnosis resting on only one of the two symptoms.\n\n**Patient S\'s situation.** Patient S exhibits symptom A but not symptom B. The requirement for diagnosis is "both symptom A and symptom B", an "and", which fails the moment either half is missing; here symptom B is missing, so the combined requirement "both symptoms present" is false for Patient S. Reading the necessary-condition arrow in its **contrapositive** form (negating and swapping both sides of an implication, which always keeps its truth value the same), not both symptoms present $\\Rightarrow$ not diagnosed with X, this directly rules out a diagnosis for Patient S. The identical reasoning would apply to anyone missing symptom A instead, since the "and" requirement fails just as completely from either missing half.\n\n**What remains possible for a patient with both symptoms.** The context\'s explicit denial of sufficiency means a patient could show both symptom A and symptom B and still not be diagnosed, if some other condition has not yet been ruled out. This gap is deliberately left open by the criterion; it is not merely unlikely, the criterion states outright that both symptoms are not enough by themselves.\n\nWith the necessary-condition arrow established for the general criterion, its explicit non-sufficiency confirmed, Patient R\'s situation resolved by applying the arrow forward, and Patient S\'s situation resolved by applying its contrapositive, every one of the five statements below is now directly answerable.',
