    difficulty_level: "3/5",
    sort_order: 19,
    solution_overview:
      'The context gives one rule with two layers, plus data on two named riders, and each layer needs to be unpacked before applying it. The rule states that the discount applies to riders who are at least 65 years old AND satisfy at least one of two hardship conditions: having a qualifying disability, OR having an annual income below \\$20,000. This has the shape of an age gate (an AND, which must always be satisfied) combined with a hardship bracket (an OR inside that gate, where either option alone is enough). Writing $A$ for "at least 65," $D$ for "qualifying disability," and $L$ for "income below \\$20,000," the rule becomes\n\n$$\\text{Discount} \\Leftrightarrow A \\land (D \\lor L)$$\n\nBecause the bracket is joined with OR, $D$ and $L$ are interchangeable substitutes for each other: satisfying either one alone is enough to make the bracket true, and satisfying both is no better than satisfying one. The age condition $A$, by contrast, sits outside the bracket, connected by AND, so nothing inside the bracket can substitute for it; a rider under 65 is disqualified no matter how strong their disability or income situation is.\n\nApplying this to Rider P: 70 years old, so $A$ is true; no disability, so $D$ is false; income \\$18,000, which is below \\$20,000, so $L$ is true. The bracket $D \\lor L$ is false OR true, which is true, and combined with $A$ true, the full condition $A \\land (D \\lor L)$ is true, so P qualifies.\n\nApplying this to Rider Q: 67 years old, so $A$ is true; qualifying disability, so $D$ is true; income \\$50,000, which is above \\$20,000, so $L$ is false. The bracket $D \\lor L$ is true OR false, which is true (the OR only needs one true side, and here the disability alone supplies it), and combined with $A$ true, the full condition is true, so Q qualifies as well, despite the failed income test.\n\nTo see the limits of the age condition, consider a hypothetical third rider: 70 years old, no disability, income \\$25,000. Here $A$ is true, but $D$ is false and $L$ is false (since \\$25,000 exceeds \\$20,000), so the bracket $D \\lor L$ is false OR false, which is false. Even though $A$ is true, the AND with a false bracket makes the whole condition false, so this rider does not qualify.\n\n| Rider | $A$ (65+) | $D$ (disability) | $L$ (income < \\$20k) | $D \\lor L$ | Discount? |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| P, age 70, no disability, \\$18,000 | true | false | true | true | yes |\n| Q, age 67, disability, \\$50,000 | true | true | false | true | yes |\n| Hypothetical, age 70, no disability, \\$25,000 | true | false | false | false | no |\n\nThis table, built directly from the rule and the two riders\' data plus one constructed test case, resolves every one of the five statements: P qualifies (statement A, true), Q qualifies despite the high income because the bracket is an OR (statement B, false, and statement C, false), the hypothetical rider shows a case of refusal despite meeting the age condition (statement D, true), and that same hypothetical rider shows age alone is not sufficient (statement E, false).',
