    difficulty_level: "3/5",
    sort_order: 23,
    solution_overview:
      'The context gives one claim from the professor: if a student studies at least 10 hours, they pass the exam. This claim has a condition, studying at least 10 hours, and an outcome, passing the exam, and it promises that the condition alone guarantees the outcome.\n\n**Building the negation.** Breaking this promise requires one very specific kind of student: someone who satisfies the condition (studied at least 10 hours) but fails to achieve the promised outcome (does not pass). This negation is a single counterexample, "studies at least 10 hours and does not pass," not another general if-then rule; any student of that exact shape would disprove the professor\'s claim, and the absence of any such student is what keeps the claim true.\n\n**Introducing Anna\'s data.** The context adds a specific student, Anna, who passes the exam without studying at all, because she already knew the material. Anna\'s shape is the reverse of the negation just built: for her, the studying condition is false (she studied zero hours, certainly less than 10) and the outcome is true (she passed). This exact combination, false condition paired with true outcome, is the key to testing every related version of the professor\'s claim.\n\n**Testing the original claim against Anna.** The professor\'s claim only makes a promise about students who do study at least 10 hours; it says nothing whatsoever about students who study less. Since Anna studied zero hours, she falls entirely outside anything the original claim addresses, so her passing without studying does not create any contradiction, and the original claim survives her case completely intact.\n\n**Testing the converse against Anna.** The converse reverses the original claim\'s direction, becoming "passing requires having studied at least 10 hours," which is effectively a claim that studying enough is the only way to pass. Anna passed without studying at all, directly contradicting that claim, so the converse is false, even while the original claim (which never made that stronger promise) remains true.\n\n**Testing the inverse against Anna.** The inverse negates both the condition and the outcome of the original claim while keeping their order, becoming "studying less than 10 hours leads to not passing." Anna studied less than 10 hours yet did pass, directly contradicting the inverse as well, so the inverse is also false.\n\n**Testing the contrapositive against Anna.** The contrapositive swaps the condition and outcome and negates both, becoming "not passing leads to not having studied at least 10 hours." Anna never failed to pass, so this contrapositive never even applies to her case, and it remains fully guaranteed by the truth of the original claim, untouched by her example.\n\nThe pattern that emerges explains all of this at once: the original claim and its contrapositive always share the same truth value (both true here), and the converse and the inverse always share the same truth value as each other (both false here, exposed by the very same counterexample, Anna), because the inverse is itself the contrapositive of the converse. Every one of the five statements above follows directly from sorting these four related claims, original, converse, inverse, and contrapositive, into their two matching pairs and checking Anna\'s data against each.',
