    solution_overview:
      "The context gives two separate pieces of information about the rule, plus two pilots' full records, and each needs unpacking before the statements can be judged.\n\n**The necessity sentence.** The first sentence says logging at least 250 flight hours is necessary for the license. “Necessary” means the license cannot be granted without it, but it does not by itself say the hours are enough on their own; something can be necessary and still fall short of sufficient.\n\n**The full requirement, stated with “if and only if.”** The second sentence sharpens this into an exact rule: the license is granted exactly when all three of the following hold at once: logging at least 250 flight hours, passing the FAA written exam, and passing the practical flight test. Because these three requirements are joined with “and,” every single one must be true for the license to be granted; a failure in any one of the three, regardless of how strong the other two are, makes the whole combination false and the license withheld. This also confirms the necessity sentence: since the hours requirement is one of three things all required together, it is indeed necessary, but it is only one third of what is actually needed, so it is not sufficient by itself.\n\n**Pilot A's record.** Pilot A logged 300 flight hours, comfortably clearing the 250 hour threshold, and passed the written exam, but failed the practical flight test. Two of the three requirements hold (hours, written exam) and one fails (practical test). Since all three are required together, this single failure is enough to make the whole combination false: Pilot A is not granted the license, regardless of having 50 hours to spare and a passed written exam.\n\n**Pilot B's record.** Pilot B logged 240 flight hours, which falls short of the 250 hour threshold by 10 hours, and passed both the written exam and the practical flight test. Here two of the three requirements hold (written exam, practical test) and the hours requirement fails, by a margin of only 10 hours. Since all three are required together, this failure alone is also enough to make the whole combination false: Pilot B is not granted the license either, despite passing both exams.\n\n**What the two records together demonstrate.** Pilot A shows that logging well over 250 hours does not by itself grant the license, since the hours requirement can hold while the license is still withheld over the practical test. This directly answers whether logging 250 or more hours by itself guarantees the license: it does not, because it is only one of three things all required together, and Pilot A has more than 250 hours without any license to show for it. Pilot A also stands as a concrete example of a pilot with more than 250 flight hours who is not granted the license.",
