    difficulty_level: "5/5",
    sort_order: 34,
    solution_overview:
      "The bank's rule combines two connectives, and reading it correctly means noticing which parts are grouped together with brackets and which are chained on outside them. The rule is: an applicant is approved if and only if [(credit score is at least 700) OR (has a qualified co-signer)], taken together, AND (debt-to-income ratio is below 40%).\n\nInside the brackets, the credit score condition and the co-signer condition are joined by “or”: satisfying either one alone is enough to clear the bracket, so a low credit score is not fatal as long as a qualified co-signer is present, and a missing co-signer is not fatal as long as the credit score clears 700. Outside the brackets, the debt-to-income ratio condition is chained on with “and”: no matter how the bracket is satisfied, the ratio condition must also hold on its own, with nothing inside the bracket able to compensate for it.\n\n**Applicant P's record.** Applicant P has a credit score of 650, which falls short of the 700 threshold, so the credit-score half of the bracket is not satisfied. But Applicant P also has a qualified co-signer, so the co-signer half of the bracket is satisfied, and since the bracket only needs one of its two halves, the bracket as a whole holds. Applicant P's debt-to-income ratio is 35%, which is below the 40% threshold, so the ratio condition outside the bracket also holds. Both the bracket and the ratio condition are satisfied, so Applicant P is approved, despite the below-700 credit score.\n\n**Applicant Q's record.** Applicant Q has a credit score of 720, which clears the 700 threshold easily, so the credit-score half of the bracket is satisfied on its own, and the bracket as a whole holds regardless of the co-signer, which Q does not have anyway. But Applicant Q's debt-to-income ratio is 45%, which is above the 40% threshold, so the ratio condition outside the bracket fails. Since the ratio condition must hold no matter how the bracket is satisfied, and here it does not, Applicant Q is denied, despite the excellent credit score.\n\n**What the two applicants together demonstrate.** Applicant P shows that a below-700 credit score is survivable when the co-signer fills the bracket's other slot and the ratio condition is separately satisfied. Applicant Q shows the opposite lesson: an excellent credit score, even at 720, cannot rescue an application once the ratio condition outside the bracket fails, since a high score fills the bracket and does nothing else, leaving the ratio condition to stand entirely on its own. Generalizing this second lesson, any applicant with a ratio of 40% or above is barred from approval no matter what is happening inside the bracket, since the ratio condition is chained on with “and” and a false “and” component makes the whole rule false regardless of everything else. Generalizing the first lesson, an applicant whose credit score is below 700 can still be approved, provided the co-signer condition fills the bracket instead and the ratio condition holds on its own, exactly as in Applicant P's case.",
