      "**D.** → True\n\nThis statement claims that clue (1), “if Ann is guilty, then Dan is innocent,” provides no additional information here, since its condition is false. Ann has already been shown innocent by combining clue (3) with the “exactly one guilty” fact, so clue (1)'s “if” half never actually holds. A conditional rule whose condition is false is automatically true but inert, contributing nothing beyond that automatic satisfaction; the conclusion that Ann is innocent was reached through the counting argument, not through clue (1). **Clue (1) adds no information in this puzzle.**",
      "**E.** → True\n\nThis statement claims that even without knowing exactly one suspect is guilty, clue (3) alone already tells us Dan is guilty. Clue (3) is not a conditional rule but a flat assertion, which needs no supporting premises, no combination with other clues, and no assumption about how many suspects are guilty overall to be read as true. The “exactly one guilty” fact was only needed afterward, to clear Ann, Ben, and Cara of guilt; it plays no role in establishing Dan's guilt itself. **Dan's guilt rests on clue (3) alone.**",
