    difficulty_level: "1/5",
    sort_order: 2,
    solution_overview:
      "Four suspects are named: Ann, Ben, Cara, and Dan, and exactly one of them committed the theft, with the other three necessarily innocent. Four numbered clues are given, and clue (3) is different in kind from the other three: it is a flat, unconditional assertion, “Dan is guilty,” rather than an if-then rule. Clues (1), (2), and (4) are all conditional rules that only make promises when their “if” half is true.\n\n**Applying clue (3) and the “exactly one” fact first.** Clue (3) states directly that Dan is guilty, with no reasoning or combination with anything else required to read it off. Combining that with the given fact that exactly one of the four suspects is guilty immediately settles the fate of the other three: since the single guilty slot is already filled by Dan, Ann, Ben, and Cara must all be innocent. This determines every suspect's status before any of the conditional clues (1), (2), or (4) even need to be consulted.\n\n**Checking clue (1) against this solution.** Clue (1) says: if Ann is guilty, then Dan is innocent. Ann has just been shown innocent by the count, so the “if” half of clue (1) is false. A conditional rule with a false “if” half is automatically true, regardless of what its “then” half says, because the rule only makes a promise when its condition actually holds; here it never gets triggered. So clue (1) is satisfied, but it contributes nothing to the reasoning, since Ann's innocence was already established purely by counting, before clue (1) was even brought in. Its contrapositive, “if Dan is guilty, then Ann is innocent,” could in principle have been used to derive Ann's innocence directly from Dan's guilt, but the counting argument already reached that conclusion by a different, more direct route, so clue (1) adds no additional information in this particular puzzle.\n\n**Checking clue (2) against this solution.** Clue (2) says: if Ben is innocent, then Cara is innocent. Ben has been shown innocent by the count, so this clue's “if” half is true, and it demands that Cara be innocent as its consequence, which matches the solution already reached. But Cara's innocence did not need clue (2) to be established: it followed the moment the count showed only Dan is guilty, leaving Ann, Ben, and Cara all innocent together, no matter what clue (2) said. Clue (2) is consistent with the solution, confirming it rather than being needed to produce it, so it is not essential to determining that Cara is innocent.\n\n**Checking clue (4) against this solution.** Clue (4) says: if Cara is guilty, then Ann is guilty. Cara has been shown innocent by the count, so this clue's “if” half is false, making it automatically true and, just like clue (1), contributing nothing further to the argument.\n\n**Summary of what each clue does and does not do.** Clue (3), a flat assertion, is the entire engine of the solution once paired with the fact that exactly one suspect is guilty; it needs no supporting premises. Clues (1), (2), and (4) are all conditional rules whose “if” halves turn out false once the count has done its work, so all three are automatically satisfied but genuinely idle: none of them is needed to reach any part of the conclusion.",
