    answer_key: [true, false, true, false, true],
    tactical_explanations: [
      "**A.** → True\n\nThis statement claims X is a truth-teller. Testing the alternative shows why: if X were a liar, then X's statement “Y always lies” would have to be false, making Y a truth-teller; but then Y's own statement, “X and I are both liars,” would have to be true, which requires Y to be a liar, directly contradicting Y being a truth-teller. Since assuming X is a liar leads to this impossible outcome, that assumption must be rejected, and the other assumption runs into no such trouble, as shown by checking it directly. **X is a truth-teller.**",
      "**B.** → False\n\nThis statement claims Y is a truth-teller. Since X has been shown to be a truth-teller, everything X says is true, including X's statement “Y always lies.” A true statement that Y always lies means Y is in fact a liar, not a truth-teller. **Y is a liar.**",
      "**C.** → True\n\nThis statement claims that Y's statement, “X and I are both liars,” is false. That statement is a joint claim requiring both halves to be true: X is a liar, and Y is a liar. The first half is false, since X has already been established as a truth-teller, and a joint “and” claim with even one false half is false as a whole. This matches exactly what should happen anyway, since Y is a liar and every statement a liar makes must be false. **Y's statement is false.**",
      "**D.** → False\n\nThis statement claims there are two equally valid solutions for the types of X and Y. Testing both possibilities for X shows this is not the case: assuming X is a liar leads directly to a contradiction, since it forces Y to be simultaneously a truth-teller and required by Y's own statement to be a liar, which cannot both hold. Only the other assignment, X truthful and Y lying, survives every consistency check. **The solution is unique, not doubled.**",
      "**E.** → True\n\nThis statement claims that if X were a liar, this would lead to a logical contradiction. Testing that assumption directly: a lying X makes X's statement “Y always lies” false, so Y would have to be a truth-teller. But a truthful Y's own statement, “X and I are both liars,” would then have to be true, which requires Y to be a liar, the exact opposite of what was just concluded. **The assumption collapses, which is precisely why that case was ruled out.**",
    ],
    difficulty_level: "1/5",
    sort_order: 3,
    solution_overview:
      "Every inhabitant of the island is either a truth-teller, whose every statement is true, or a liar, whose every statement is false; there is no third option, and no inhabitant is a mix of both. Two statements are given: X says, “Y always lies,” and Y says, “X and I are both liars.”\n\nSince X must be one or the other, the two possibilities for X, truth-teller or liar, can each be tested in turn to see which one is actually consistent with everything.\n\n**Testing the possibility that X is a liar.** If X is a liar, then everything X says is false, so X's statement “Y always lies” must be false, which means Y is not a liar, so Y must be a truth-teller. But if Y is a truth-teller, everything Y says must be true, so Y's statement, “X and I are both liars,” must be true. That statement claims both X is a liar and Y is a liar. The first half matches the case being tested, so far so good. But the second half claims Y is a liar, which directly contradicts what was just concluded, that Y is a truth-teller. A truth-teller cannot be truthfully saying that they themselves are a liar. So assuming X is a liar leads to a genuine contradiction, and this possibility must be rejected.\n\n**Testing the possibility that X is a truth-teller.** If X is a truth-teller, then everything X says is true, so X's statement “Y always lies” must be true, meaning Y is indeed a liar. Since Y is a liar, everything Y says must be false, so Y's statement, “X and I are both liars,” must be false. That statement is a joint claim requiring both halves (X is a liar, and Y is a liar) to be true for the whole statement to be true; since a joint claim with “and” is false as soon as one of its halves is false, and here the first half is false, because X has just been established as a truth-teller, the whole statement is indeed false, exactly as required for a liar's statement to be. Nothing here clashes: X being truthful is consistent with X's own statement being true, and Y being a liar is consistent with Y's own statement being false. This possibility survives every check.\n\n**Reaching the unique answer.** One of the two possibilities for X collapses into contradiction, and the other survives every consistency check, so the case that survives is the only correct assignment: X is a truth-teller, and Y is a liar. There is no second valid solution, since the only alternative was already ruled impossible. As a direct consequence, Y's statement, “X and I are both liars,” is false, both because it is a liar's statement and because its first half, X is a liar, is directly false on its own.",
