      "**A.** → True\n\nThis statement claims the candidate described (R1 and R2 voting yes, R3 and R4 voting no) is approved. This gives exactly two yes-votes. The first path, needing at least three yes-votes, is closed, since two is fewer than three. The second path, exactly two yes-votes with R1 among them, is open, since there are exactly two yes-votes and R1 is indeed one of the two. **Approved.**",
      '**B.** → False\n\nThis statement claims that if R2 and R3 had voted yes instead, with R1 and R4 voting no, the candidate would still be approved. This scenario again gives exactly two yes-votes, so the first path stays closed just as before. The trap is treating the tie-breaking power as belonging to "any two reviewers"; it is written for R1 by name, and here R1 voted no, with the two yes-votes belonging to R2 and R3 instead, so the second path is closed too. **Rejected, not approved.**',
      "**C.** → True\n\nThis statement claims that if exactly three reviewers vote yes, the candidate is always approved, regardless of which three they are. The first path only counts yes-votes and places no requirement on which specific reviewers make up that count; it opens whenever the count reaches at least three, and exactly three yes-votes always satisfies that. **All four possible triples give approval.**",
      "**D.** → False\n\nThis statement claims R1's yes-vote is necessary for approval in every possible scenario. \"Necessary in every scenario\" is a strong claim, and one counter-scenario sinks it: yes-votes from R2, R3 and R4 give a count of 3, opening the first path on count alone, with R1 voting no throughout. R1's vote is decisive only in the narrower two-vote case. **Not necessary.**",
