    tactical_explanations: [
      '**A.** → True\n\nThis statement claims J is a liar. Testing the alternative shows why: if J told the truth, J\'s own claim "exactly one of us is a truth-teller" would have to be accurate, and since J would be that one truth-teller, both K and L would have to be liars. But then L\'s sentence, "K and I are the same type," would describe reality correctly, since both K and L really would be liars of the same type, and a liar can never say something accurate. That contradiction kills the branch where J is truthful, leaving only the branch where J lies. **The statement is true.**',
      "**B.** → True\n\nThis statement claims K is a truth-teller. K's sentence is \"J is lying,\" and statement A has just established that J really is lying. A sentence that correctly describes reality can only be spoken by a truth-teller, since a liar's sentence must be false, so someone whose sentence matches the facts cannot be a liar. Because K's sentence matches the fact that J lies, K must be the truth-teller who said it. **The statement is true.**",
      "**C.** → True\n\nThis statement claims L is a truth-teller. Since J is a liar (statement A), J's sentence \"exactly one of us is a truth-teller\" must itself be false, so the actual number of truth-tellers among the three is not one. Statement B already secures K as one confirmed truth-teller, so the true count must be at least two, and L is the only remaining inhabitant who could supply that second truth-teller. Checking L's own sentence under that assignment confirms it is consistent: L says \"K and I are the same type,\" and with K a truth-teller and L a truth-teller, they are indeed the same type, so L's sentence is accurate, exactly as a truth-teller's sentence should be. **The statement is true.**",
      "**D.** → False\n\nThis statement repeats J's own claim, that exactly one of the three inhabitants is a truth-teller. But J has already been shown to be a liar (statement A), which forces J's sentence to be false rather than true. The actual situation, worked out in statements B and C, has two truth-tellers, K and L, with only J lying, so the true count is two, not one. **The statement is false.**",
      '**E.** → False\n\nThis statement asks whether some other combination of types, besides "J liar, K and L truth-tellers," could also satisfy all three sentences. The branch where J tells the truth was already shown to self-destruct under statement A, since it forces L into saying something accurate while being a liar, which is impossible. Within the surviving branch where J lies, K is forced to be a truth-teller by statement B, and the requirement that the true count not equal one, combined with L\'s own sentence needing to be accurate as a truth-teller, forces L to be a truth-teller too, with no freedom left to make L a liar instead: doing so would drop the truth-teller count back to exactly one, reviving the very contradiction the branch was built to avoid. Every alternative assignment breaks at least one of the three sentences. **The statement is false.**',
    ],
    difficulty_level: "3/5",
    sort_order: 22,
    solution_overview:
      "Three inhabitants live on an island where every truth-teller's sentence is true and every liar's sentence is false, with no islander mixing the two. Three sentences are given, and it helps to treat each as its own clue before combining them.\n\n**Clue 1 (J's sentence): \"Exactly one of us is a truth-teller.\"** This is a claim about the total headcount of truth-tellers among all three inhabitants. If J is a truth-teller, this headcount really is one, and J is that one. If J is a liar, the headcount is anything except one, meaning zero, two, or three.\n\n**Clue 2 (K's sentence): \"J is lying.\"** This is a claim purely about J's type. If K is a truth-teller, J really is lying. If K is a liar, J is actually telling the truth. Either way, this sentence locks J and K into opposite types: whichever one J turns out to be, K is forced to be the other, because K's sentence is accurate exactly when J is a liar and inaccurate exactly when J is a truth-teller.\n\n**Clue 3 (L's sentence): \"K and I are the same type.\"** This is a claim about whether K and L match. If L is a truth-teller, K and L really are the same type (both truth-tellers, since L is one). If L is a liar, K and L are actually different types, meaning K would have to be a truth-teller while L is a liar.\n\nWith the meaning of each clue in hand, there are only two branches to check, split on J.\n\n**Branch 1: J is a truth-teller.** Clue 1 then has to be accurate, so exactly one of the three is a truth-teller, and since J is a truth-teller, J is that one, forcing K and L to both be liars. Now check clue 3 under this assignment: L is a liar, so L's sentence must be false, meaning K and L should actually be different types. But both were just placed in the liar category, which makes them the same type, not different. That mismatch between what clue 3 requires (K and L different, since L is a liar) and what the branch actually produces (K and L both liars, hence the same) is a direct contradiction. **Branch 1 is impossible.**\n\n**Branch 2: J is a liar.** By clue 2's link between J and K, K's sentence \"J is lying\" is now an accurate description of reality, so K must be a truth-teller. Because J is a liar, clue 1's content is false, so the true headcount of truth-tellers is not one; it must be zero, two, or three. K alone already accounts for one truth-teller, so the headcount is at least one, ruling out zero, and it cannot equal one either (that is exactly what was just excluded), so it must be two or three. Since J is fixed as a liar, three truth-tellers is impossible, leaving two as the only option, which means the second truth-teller has to be L. Checking clue 3 under L as a truth-teller confirms consistency: it requires K and L to be the same type, and both are now truth-tellers, so the sentence is accurate, exactly as required of a truth-teller.\n\n**The unique solution:** J is a liar, K is a truth-teller, and L is a truth-teller. Every clue checks out: clue 1 (J, a liar, claims exactly one truth-teller, while the real count is two, so the false sentence is correctly false), clue 2 (K, a truth-teller, claims J is lying, which is true), and clue 3 (L, a truth-teller, claims K and L match, which is true since both are truth-tellers). With branch 1 eliminated and branch 2 pinned down at every step by clues 1 through 3, no other assignment of types can satisfy all three sentences at once.",
