      '**A.** → True\n\nThis statement claims Dmitri attends the conference no matter which valid roster you look at. Rule (4) states this directly as a fact about the situation, "Dmitri attends," rather than as a conditional that needs to be triggered, so it applies unconditionally to every roster consistent with all four rules. Both rosters found in the overview, {Ana, Ceci, Dmitri} and {Boris, Dmitri}, indeed contain Dmitri. **The statement is true.**',
      "**B.** → False\n\nThis statement claims the four rules pin Ana down to a single forced value, either always attending or always absent. But the case analysis in the overview produced two complete, valid rosters: Case A has Ana attending alongside Ceci and Dmitri, while Case B has Ana absent alongside Boris and Dmitri. Since Ana attends in one legitimate solution and does not attend in another, no single value is forced on her, and the rules leave her attendance genuinely open. **The statement is false.**",
      "**C.** → True\n\nThis statement claims that some valid roster has exactly three of the four colleagues attending. Case A supplies exactly that: Ana attends by assumption, rule (1) sends Boris away, rule (2) brings Ceci in once Boris is confirmed absent, and rule (4) guarantees Dmitri, giving the three person roster {Ana, Ceci, Dmitri} with only Boris missing. A claim that something is merely possible only needs one confirmed example, and this roster, already checked against all four rules, is exactly that example. **The statement is true.**",
