    solution_overview:
      "Four colleagues are each either attending or not, and four rules constrain the outcome. Rule (4) is not a conditional at all but a flat statement of fact, \"Dmitri attends,\" so it holds in absolutely every valid roster with no case work needed: Dmitri is always present. Rule (1) says Ana attending forces Boris to stay away, but says nothing about what happens when Ana is absent. Rule (2) says Boris's absence forces Ceci in, but says nothing when Boris is present. Rule (3) is the one rule with a built in exception: normally Ceci attending forces Dmitri out, but that requirement is switched off whenever Ana also attends, in which case Dmitri's attendance is left completely unrestricted by this rule. Because rule (3) behaves differently depending on Ana, and none of the other three rules pin Ana down directly, the natural way to solve the puzzle is to split into two cases based on Ana.\n\n**Case A: Ana attends.** Rule (1) triggers immediately, since its condition (Ana attends) is met, so Boris does not attend. With Boris now confirmed absent, rule (2)'s condition (Boris does not attend) is met, so Ceci attends. Now check rule (3): Ceci attends, which would normally force Dmitri out, but the exception is active here because Ana attends, so rule (3) imposes no restriction on Dmitri at all. Rule (4) still guarantees Dmitri attends regardless, and rule (3)'s exception means there is no conflict. The resulting roster is **Ana, Ceci, Dmitri**, with Boris the only one absent, three attendees in total. Checking all four rules against this roster confirms it is fully valid.\n\n**Case B: Ana does not attend.** Rule (1)'s condition is not met, so it imposes nothing here. Rule (3) no longer has its exception available, since that exception only activates when Ana attends, so rule (3) now applies in its plain, unconditional form: Ceci attending would force Dmitri out. Rule (4) guarantees Dmitri attends, and Dmitri attending is incompatible with Ceci also attending under this plain form, so Ceci must be absent. With Ceci now confirmed absent, rule (2) read in its contrapositive direction (if Ceci does not attend, then Boris cannot be absent either, since rule (2) says Boris's absence would have forced Ceci in) requires Boris to attend. The resulting roster is **Boris, Dmitri**, with Ana and Ceci both absent, two attendees in total. Checking all four rules against this roster confirms it too is fully valid.\n\n**Two solutions, one open variable.** Both cases produce a complete, rule-consistent roster, so there are exactly two valid solutions overall, and they disagree only about Ana (and correspondingly about Boris and Ceci, which are chained to her through rules (1) and (2)); Dmitri is the one name common to both. Because a genuine choice survives after applying every rule, none of Ana, Boris, or Ceci is uniquely forced, only Dmitri is. Comparing the two rosters directly also shows that Ana and Boris are never absent together: in Case A, Ana attends and Boris is the one absent, while in Case B, Ana is absent and Boris attends instead, so at least one of the two is always present. Finally, since Case B contains no Ceci at all, removing rule (3)'s exception would only threaten Case A (the one roster where Ceci and Dmitri actually attend together); Case B would remain valid exactly as before, so the rule set as a whole would stay satisfiable even without the exception, just with fewer solutions.",
