      "**A.** → True\n\nThis statement claims that Noah joining forces Zoe to stay out. Rule (4) says the relationship in the other direction, that Zoe joining forces Noah not to join, written as Zoe implies not Noah. Reversing an implication's hypothesis and conclusion while negating both, forming its contrapositive, always keeps the same truth value, and that contrapositive is exactly Noah implies not Zoe, which is the statement being tested. The single roster containing Noah, {Noah, Maria}, confirms it directly as well, since it has no Zoe in it. **The statement is true.**",
      "**B.** → False\n\nThis statement claims Leo and Noah could join the session together. Follow the chain of consequences: rule (2) says Leo joining forces Zoe to join as well, and rule (4) says Zoe joining forces Noah to stay out. Chaining these two together, Leo joining forces Zoe joining forces Noah staying out, so Leo's presence and Noah's presence can never coexist in any roster satisfying rules (2) and (4). Checking the five valid rosters directly confirms this: none of them lists both Leo and Noah at once. **The statement is false.**",
      "**C.** → True\n\nThis statement claims that Maria can join while Leo stays out. The roster {Maria} alone demonstrates this is possible: with Noah, Leo, and Zoe all absent, rule (1)'s condition (Noah joins) is false so it demands nothing, rule (2)'s condition (Leo joins) is false so it demands nothing, rule (4)'s condition (Zoe joins) is false so it demands nothing, and rule (3) (Maria or Leo joins) is satisfied because Maria alone is present. Since this roster is fully valid and has Maria without Leo, the possibility is confirmed. **The statement is true.**",
      '**D.** → True\n\nThis statement claims that if Maria does not join, then Noah does not join either. This is exactly the contrapositive of rule (1) (Noah implies Maria): swapping and negating the two conditions turns "Noah joining forces Maria to join" into "Maria not joining forces Noah not to join," and a contrapositive always shares the truth value of its original rule. Since rule (1) is a given rule that always holds, its contrapositive holds automatically in every roster, with no case work needed. **The statement is true.**',
