    solution_overview:
      "Four students and four rules govern who may join the study session, and it helps to spell out what each rule means before combining them. Rule (1) says that whenever Noah joins, Maria must join too, but it says nothing at all about what happens if Noah stays home. Rule (2) says that Leo may join only if Zoe joins, meaning Leo joining forces Zoe to join, though again it places no requirement on Zoe when Leo is absent. Rule (3) is an existence requirement: at least one of Maria or Leo must join, with no upper limit, so both could join together or just one could. Rule (4) says that whenever Zoe joins, Noah must not join, but it says nothing when Zoe stays home. A rule whose \"if\" condition is false simply has nothing to say and is automatically satisfied.\n\n**A chain worth spotting before case work.** Rule (2) says Leo joining forces Zoe joining, and rule (4) says Zoe joining forces Noah not joining. Chaining these two rules together: Leo joining forces Zoe joining forces Noah not joining. So in any roster where Leo is present, Noah must be absent, meaning Leo and Noah can never both join the same session.\n\n**Case 1: Noah joins.** Rule (1)'s condition is met, so Maria must join as well. Rule (4) read in its contrapositive form (Noah joining forces Zoe not joining, exactly the reverse-and-negate of \"Zoe joining forces Noah not joining\") means Zoe is excluded. With Zoe excluded, rule (2) read in its contrapositive form (Zoe not joining forces Leo not joining, since Leo joining would have required Zoe) excludes Leo too. Rule (3) is already satisfied by Maria's presence. This produces exactly one roster in this case: **{Noah, Maria}**.\n\n**Case 2: Noah does not join.** Rule (1)'s condition is false, so it demands nothing further, and rule (4)'s conclusion (Noah does not join) is already true regardless of Zoe, so rule (4) places no restriction on Zoe here either. Rule (3) still requires Maria or Leo, and rule (2) still requires that Leo joining brings Zoe along. Checking every combination of Maria, Leo, and Zoe against these two remaining live rules:\n\n| Maria | Leo | Zoe | Rule (3): Maria or Leo | Rule (2): Leo needs Zoe | Verdict |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| No | No | No | fails | | invalid |\n| No | No | Yes | fails | | invalid |\n| No | Yes | No | holds | fails | invalid |\n| No | Yes | Yes | holds | holds | valid: {Leo, Zoe} |\n| Yes | No | No | holds | | valid: {Maria} |\n| Yes | No | Yes | holds | | valid: {Maria, Zoe} |\n| Yes | Yes | No | holds | fails | invalid |\n| Yes | Yes | Yes | holds | holds | valid: {Maria, Leo, Zoe} |\n\nFour valid rosters survive in this case: **{Leo, Zoe}**, **{Maria}**, **{Maria, Zoe}**, and **{Maria, Leo, Zoe}**.\n\n**Five valid rosters overall.** Combining both cases gives five legal rosters in total: {Noah, Maria}, {Maria}, {Maria, Zoe}, {Maria, Leo, Zoe}, and {Leo, Zoe}. No single student appears in every one of the five (Maria is missing from {Leo, Zoe}, and Zoe is missing from {Noah, Maria} and {Maria}), so no student, Zoe included, is actually forced to join every valid session; Maria comes closest, appearing in four of the five, but even she is optional when Leo and Zoe join together without her.",
