    solution_overview:
      "Three roommates and four rules decide who cooks each night, and the first two rules interact directly with each other. Rule (1) says that whenever Owen cooks, Priya must not cook, which forbids the two of them from cooking together. Rule (2) says at least one of Owen or Priya must cook, which forbids the two of them from both sitting out. Combining these two rules, since they can never both cook and can never both abstain, exactly one of Owen and Priya must cook on any given night, no other option is available. Rule (3) says that whenever Priya cooks, Quinn must cook too, but says nothing when Priya does not cook. Rule (4) says Quinn cooks only if Owen does not, meaning Quinn cooking forces Owen not to cook, though it places no requirement on Owen when Quinn is absent. With exactly one of Owen and Priya always cooking, there are only two evenings to check.\n\n**Case 1: Owen cooks.** Rule (1) forces Priya not to cook. Rule (3)'s condition (Priya cooks) is now false, so it demands nothing about Quinn. What about Quinn independently? Rule (4) says Quinn cooking would force Owen not to cook, but Owen is cooking in this case, so by that rule's contrapositive (Owen cooking forces Quinn not to cook), Quinn must not cook either. The result is Owen cooking alone: **{Owen}**.\n\n**Case 2: Priya cooks.** Rule (1)'s condition (Owen cooks) is now false, so it demands nothing, meaning Owen is simply not cooking in this case (consistent with exactly one of Owen and Priya cooking). Rule (3)'s condition is now true, so Quinn must cook. Checking rule (4): Quinn is cooking, and the rule requires that Owen not cook when Quinn does, which matches, since Owen is already absent from this case. The result is Priya and Quinn cooking together: **{Priya, Quinn}**.\n\n**The complete list of two valid arrangements: {Owen} and {Priya, Quinn}.** Notice what this list rules out directly: an evening where Quinn cooks alone while neither Owen nor Priya does would violate rule (2), which always requires one of those two in the kitchen, so Quinn can never be the sole cook. And since Owen appears in only one of the two arrangements while Priya (with Quinn) appears in the other, neither Owen nor Quinn is obliged to cook on any given night; only the requirement that one of Owen or Priya cooks is truly unavoidable.",
