      "**C.** → True\n\nThis statement claims Hugo is required to go on every valid trip. Rule (4) requires at least one of Diego or Hugo to go, and statement A has already shown Diego can never go in any valid scenario, so Diego can never be the one satisfying rule (4). The entire burden of rule (4) therefore falls on Hugo in every case, meaning Hugo must go regardless of what anyone else decides. All four valid rosters, {Hugo}, {Hugo, Grace}, {Hugo, Iris}, and {Hugo, Grace, Iris}, confirm this directly. **The statement is true.**",
      "**D.** → True\n\nThis statement claims Grace and Iris could both attend at the same time. With Diego and Fatima both excluded from every scenario (statements A and B's discussion), rule (1)'s condition (Diego goes) is always false and demands nothing, rule (3)'s condition (Fatima goes) is always false and demands nothing, and rule (5)'s condition (Iris goes) leads only to \"Diego does not go,\" which is already guaranteed regardless of Iris. Rule (2) (Grace or Hugo) is already satisfied by Hugo alone, since Hugo always goes (statement C), so it places no further requirement on Grace. No rule ties Grace to Iris in either direction, so the roster {Hugo, Grace, Iris} satisfies all six rules. **The statement is true.**",
      '**E.** → False\n\nThis statement claims that if Iris goes, then Fatima goes too. But statement A already established that Fatima never goes in any valid scenario, since the chain through rules (1), (3), (2), and (6) rules out Diego and Fatima together, while Iris does go in two of the four valid rosters, {Hugo, Iris} and {Hugo, Grace, Iris}. Taking the roster {Hugo, Grace, Iris} as a direct test: the "if" part of the statement (Iris goes) is satisfied, but the "then" part (Fatima goes) is not, since Fatima is absent from every roster, and an implication with a true condition and a false conclusion is exactly what makes it false. **The statement is false.**',
