    solution_overview:
      "Five club members and six rules decide who joins the day trip, so each rule's meaning is worth spelling out first. Rule (1) says Diego going forces Fatima to go, with nothing said when Diego stays home. Rule (2), \"Grace goes unless Hugo goes,\" means that whenever Hugo does not go, Grace must go instead, which is equivalent to saying at least one of Grace or Hugo always goes (both could go too). Rule (3) says Fatima going forces Grace not to go, with nothing said when Fatima stays home. Rule (4) is a plain existence requirement: at least one of Diego or Hugo must go. Rule (5) says Iris going forces Diego not to go, with nothing said when Iris stays home. Rule (6) says Hugo goes only if Fatima does not, meaning Hugo going forces Fatima not to go, with nothing said when Hugo stays home.\n\n**Testing whether Diego can go.** Suppose Diego goes. Rule (1) then forces Fatima to go as well. Rule (3), now triggered because Fatima is going, forces Grace not to go. Rule (2) needs Grace or Hugo, and Grace has just been excluded, so Hugo must go to keep rule (2) satisfied. But rule (6), now triggered because Hugo is going, forces Fatima not to go, directly contradicting the Fatima going that rule (1) produced at the start of this very chain. The assumption that Diego goes leads, through rules (1), (3), (2), and (6) in that order, to Fatima both going and not going at once, which is impossible. So **Diego never goes in any valid scenario**.\n\n**What follows immediately from Diego's exclusion.** Rule (4) requires Diego or Hugo, and Diego is permanently unavailable, so **Hugo must go in every valid scenario**. Rule (6), now always triggered because Hugo always goes, forces **Fatima to stay out in every valid scenario**. With Diego and Fatima both permanently excluded, rule (1)'s condition is always false and demands nothing further, rule (3)'s condition is always false and demands nothing further, and rule (5)'s conclusion (Diego does not go) is already guaranteed on its own, so rule (5) places no actual restriction on Iris either way.\n\n**What remains genuinely free.** Rule (2) is already satisfied by Hugo's permanent presence, so it imposes nothing further on Grace. No surviving rule connects Grace to Iris in either direction, so each of them may independently go or stay home, giving four combinations once Hugo is added back in every time:\n\n$$\\{\\text{Hugo}\\}, \\quad \\{\\text{Hugo}, \\text{Grace}\\}, \\quad \\{\\text{Hugo}, \\text{Iris}\\}, \\quad \\{\\text{Hugo}, \\text{Grace}, \\text{Iris}\\}.$$\n\nFour valid rosters in total, every one containing Hugo, none containing Diego or Fatima, and each one checked against all six rules with no violations.",
