    difficulty_level: "5/5",
    sort_order: 29,
    solution_overview:
      "Five researchers and six rules decide who reviews the paper, and it is worth restating each rule's meaning before combining them. Rule (1) says Petra reviewing forces Quinn not to review, with nothing said when Petra does not review. Rule (2), \"Ravi reviews unless Sana reviews,\" means that whenever Sana does not review, Ravi must review instead, equivalent to saying at least one of Ravi or Sana always reviews. Rule (3) says that whenever Quinn does not review, Theo must review, with nothing said when Quinn does review. Rule (4) is a plain existence requirement: at least one of Petra or Sana must review. Rule (5) says Theo reviewing forces Ravi not to review, with nothing said when Theo does not review. Rule (6) says Sana reviews only if Petra reviews, meaning Sana reviewing requires Petra to review, with nothing said when Sana does not review.\n\n**The question the rules answer most sharply: does Petra review?** Suppose she does not. Rule (6) says Sana reviewing requires Petra to review, so if Petra does not review, Sana cannot review either (this is rule (6)'s contrapositive: Petra not reviewing forces Sana not to review). But rule (4) demands at least one of Petra or Sana review, and the assumption has just eliminated both of them at once, a direct contradiction. So the assumption fails, and **Petra reviews in every valid assignment**.\n\n**From there, every remaining status is forced in sequence, one rule at a time:**\n\n• **Rule (1):** Petra reviews, so Quinn does not review.\n\n• **Rule (3):** Quinn does not review, so Theo reviews.\n\n• **Rule (5):** Theo reviews, so Ravi does not review.\n\n• **Rule (2):** Ravi does not review, so rule (2)'s requirement (Ravi or Sana) can only be met by Sana, forcing Sana to review.\n\n• **Rule (6):** Sana reviews, and this rule requires Petra to review whenever Sana does, which is already true, so this step introduces no new information and simply confirms consistency.\n\n**The resulting assignment: Petra, Sana, and Theo review the paper; Quinn and Ravi do not.** Checking every rule against this assignment confirms it satisfies all six with no conflicts. More importantly, at no step in the chain above did two different options remain open, every status followed by strict necessity from an earlier one, so this assignment is not merely one valid solution among several but the **only** one the six rules allow.",
