    difficulty_level: "5/5",
    sort_order: 30,
    solution_overview:
      "Rule (8) sets a floor of at least four competitors, so the whole argument revolves around counting who is forced in and who is forced out, and then checking every remaining rule against that count. It helps to go through the eight rules once, one at a time, before combining them.\n\n**Rule (1)** ties Uma and Victor together: Uma competes exactly when Victor does, and Uma stays out exactly when Victor stays out. **Rule (2)** says Victor's presence drags Wendy in as well, though it says nothing about what happens when Victor is absent. **Rule (3)** is a strict either or between Wendy and Xavier: one of them is always in and the other is always out, so knowing one immediately tells you the other. **Rule (4)** says Xavier's presence pushes Yara out, but again gives no information when Xavier is absent. **Rule (5)** guarantees that Yara and Zane are not both absent at once. **Rule (6)** says Zane can only join when Bianca stays home, which is the same as saying Zane and Bianca can never both compete. **Rule (7)** says Bianca's presence forces Uma in, but is automatically satisfied whenever Uma is already competing for some other reason. **Rule (8)**, the headcount rule, is the constraint that decides which branches of the puzzle can even survive.\n\n**Testing whether Victor can sit out.** Suppose Victor does not compete. Rule (1) then removes Uma as well, since the two rise and fall together. Reading rule (7) in its contrapositive form, no Uma means no Bianca, so Bianca is out too. That leaves only Wendy, Xavier, Yara and Zane still undecided, and rule (3) forces exactly one of Wendy and Xavier.\n\n• If Wendy is the one who competes and Xavier stays out, rule (4) has nothing to say, since its trigger, Xavier competing, never happens. Rule (5) still asks for Yara or Zane, so the largest this branch can ever become is Wendy plus Yara plus Zane, three people in total, which never reaches the four that rule (8) demands.\n\n• If Xavier is the one who competes and Wendy stays out, rule (4) removes Yara. Rule (5) then needs Zane to fill in for the missing Yara. The roster in this branch is only Xavier and Zane, two people, far short of four.\n\nBoth ways of losing Victor collapse under rule (8), so **Victor has to compete in every valid roster**, which settles statement A as true.\n\n**What Victor's presence forces next.** With Victor competing, rule (1) brings Uma in as well. Rule (2) brings Wendy in. Rule (3) then has already used up its one allowed slot on Wendy, so Xavier is locked out for good, in every single valid roster, with no exceptions. That is exactly what statement B denies is possible, so **statement B is false**: Xavier can never take part, not in this roster or any other.\n\n**Where things stand after the forced moves.** Uma, Victor and Wendy are now permanently in, and Xavier is permanently out, in every valid roster. That is three confirmed competitors, so rule (8) still needs at least one more name from the undecided group of Yara, Zane and Bianca. Because Xavier is out, rule (4)'s trigger never fires, so it places no further restriction on Yara here. Because Uma is already competing, rule (7)'s conclusion is already satisfied no matter what Bianca decides, so rule (7) also drops out of the picture from this point on. That leaves only rule (5) and rule (6) still doing active work among Yara, Zane and Bianca, together with the headcount floor from rule (8).\n\n**Checking every combination of the last three people.** There are eight ways to decide competing or not for Yara, Zane and Bianca, and each can be checked against rule (5), at least one of Yara or Zane, and rule (6), never both Zane and Bianca:\n\n| Yara | Zane | Bianca | Rule (5) | Rule (6) | Verdict |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| No | No | No | fails | | invalid |\n| Yes | No | No | holds | holds | valid, roster size 4 |\n| No | Yes | No | holds | holds | valid, roster size 4 |\n| No | No | Yes | fails | | invalid |\n| Yes | Yes | No | holds | holds | valid, roster size 5 |\n| Yes | No | Yes | holds | holds | valid, roster size 5 |\n| No | Yes | Yes | holds | fails | invalid |\n| Yes | Yes | Yes | holds | fails | invalid |\n\nFour combinations survive, giving four complete valid rosters once Uma, Victor, Wendy and Xavier are added back in: the base trio plus Yara alone, the base trio plus Zane alone, the base trio plus Yara and Zane together, and the base trio plus Yara and Bianca together. The surviving sizes are four, four, five and five, and every rule from (1) through (8) has now been checked against each of them.\n\n**Reading off the remaining statements from that table.** Statement C claims that Bianca competing forces Zane out, and that is exactly rule (6) written in its other direction: rule (6) already says Zane competing forces Bianca out, and flipping an implication around its two halves in this way always produces a logically equivalent statement, its contrapositive. The single row where Bianca competes, Yara together with Bianca, indeed has Zane absent, confirming it directly as well, so **statement C is true**.\n\nStatement D claims the puzzle has exactly one solution, but the table above lists four different valid rosters, so **statement D is false**; the rules pin down Uma, Victor, Wendy and Xavier completely but leave real freedom over the other three names.\n\nStatement E asks whether exactly six of the seven finalists could ever compete together. Since Xavier is permanently excluded, six competitors would require every one of the other six, Uma, Victor, Wendy, Yara, Zane and Bianca, to compete at the same time. That would need Yara, Zane and Bianca all in together, and the table already marks that exact combination invalid because rule (6) forbids Zane and Bianca from coexisting. So no roster of size six exists, the largest possible rosters top out at five, and **statement E is false**.",
